// Lesson 1: Introduction to Business Environments and the Micro Environment
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Introduction to Business Environments\n\nEvery business, whether a small spaza shop in a township or a large corporation listed on the JSE, operates within an environment. The **business environment** refers to all the internal and external factors that influence the way a business operates.\n\nThe business environment is divided into three layers:\n\n| Layer | Also Called | Level of Control |\n|-------|-----------|------------------|\n| **Micro environment** | Internal environment | Business CAN control |\n| **Market environment** | Task/operating environment | Business has SOME influence |\n| **Macro environment** | External/remote environment | Business CANNOT control |\n\nUnderstanding the business environment is essential because it helps business owners and managers to make informed decisions, plan for the future, and respond to changes effectively.'),
  t(2, '### The Micro Environment\n\nThe micro environment consists of all the **internal factors** that the business **can control** or influence directly. These factors exist inside the business and can be changed by management decisions.\n\n**Components of the micro environment:**\n\n| Component | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Vision and mission** | The purpose and long-term direction of the business |\n| **Business goals and objectives** | What the business wants to achieve |\n| **Organisational culture** | The shared values, beliefs, and behaviour within the business |\n| **Management and leadership** | The people who plan, organise, lead, and control |\n| **Organisational resources** | The physical, financial, human, and technological assets |\n| **Organisational structure** | How the business is arranged and how authority flows |\n| **Business functions** | The eight key activities performed by the business |'),
  t(3, '### Vision, Mission, and Goals\n\n**Vision statement:** A short description of the ideal future the business wants to create. It answers: "Where do we want to be?"\n- Example: Shoprite — "To be Africa\'s most accessible and affordable retailer"\n\n**Mission statement:** Describes the business\'s purpose and how it will achieve its vision. It answers: "Why do we exist and how do we operate?"\n- Example: FNB — "To be a great business helping to create a better world"\n\n**Goals** are broad, long-term outcomes the business wants to achieve.\n- Example: "Increase market share in the Western Cape"\n\n**Objectives** are specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART) targets:\n- Example: "Increase market share in the Western Cape by 3% within the next 12 months"\n\nThe vision guides the mission, which guides the goals, which guide the objectives. Together they give the business direction and purpose.'),
  q(4, 'Which component of the micro environment describes the ideal future the business wants to create?',
    ['Mission statement', 'Vision statement', 'Organisational culture', 'Business objectives'], 1,
    'The vision statement describes the ideal future the business wants to create. The mission statement explains the purpose and how the business will achieve that vision.'),
  t(5, '### Organisational Culture\n\nOrganisational culture is the "personality" of the business. It includes the shared values, beliefs, norms, and practices that guide how employees behave and work together.\n\n**Elements of organisational culture:**\n- How employees communicate (formally or informally)\n- Dress code (formal business attire vs casual)\n- Level of innovation encouraged\n- How mistakes are handled (learning vs punishment)\n- Work-life balance expectations\n- The physical workspace (open plan vs private offices)\n\n**Examples of different cultures:**\n- **Nando\'s** has a fun, creative culture with colourful restaurants, humorous advertising, and a relaxed work environment\n- **A corporate law firm** typically has a formal culture with strict dress codes, hierarchical communication, and structured working hours\n\n**Why organisational culture matters:**\n- Attracts and retains talented employees\n- Affects employee motivation and productivity\n- Shapes how the business is perceived by customers\n- Influences decision-making and innovation'),
  fb(6, 'The micro environment consists of ___ factors that the business ___ control directly.',
    ['internal', 'can'],
    'The micro environment is internal to the business and includes factors that management can directly control or influence.',
    ['Think about where these factors exist — inside or outside the business.']),
  t(7, '### Organisational Resources\n\nEvery business needs resources to operate. These include:\n\n| Resource Type | Description | Examples |\n|--------------|-------------|----------|\n| **Human resources** | The people who work in the business | Employees, managers, directors |\n| **Financial resources** | Money available to the business | Capital, profits, loans, investments |\n| **Physical resources** | Tangible assets used in operations | Buildings, vehicles, equipment, stock |\n| **Technological resources** | Systems and tools that improve efficiency | Computers, software, machinery, internet |\n| **Information resources** | Data and knowledge used for decisions | Market research, financial reports, customer databases |\n\n**Example:** A small bakery in Pretoria needs human resources (bakers, cashiers), financial resources (capital to buy ingredients), physical resources (ovens, counter space, packaging), and technological resources (a point-of-sale system and social media accounts).'),
  q(8, 'Which of the following is an example of a technological resource?',
    ['A delivery van', 'A bank loan', 'Point-of-sale software', 'An employee'], 2,
    'Point-of-sale software is a technological resource. A delivery van is a physical resource, a bank loan is a financial resource, and an employee is a human resource.'),
  t(9, '### Organisational Structure\n\nOrganisational structure defines how the business is arranged, who reports to whom, and how authority flows.\n\n**Key terms:**\n- **Organogram** — A diagram showing the structure of the business\n- **Chain of command** — The line of authority from top management to the lowest level\n- **Span of control** — The number of employees a manager directly supervises\n- **Centralisation** — Decision-making authority is concentrated at the top\n- **Decentralisation** — Decision-making authority is spread across different levels\n\n**Types of organisational structures:**\n\n| Structure | Description | Best For |\n|-----------|-------------|----------|\n| **Flat** | Few management levels, wide span of control | Small businesses, startups |\n| **Tall** | Many management levels, narrow span of control | Large businesses, banks |\n| **Functional** | Organised by business function (marketing, finance, HR) | Medium to large businesses |\n| **Divisional** | Organised by product, region, or customer type | Large national/multinational businesses |'),
  q(10, 'What does "span of control" refer to in an organisational structure?',
    ['The number of management levels in the business', 'The number of employees a manager directly supervises', 'The total number of employees in the business', 'The geographic area the business covers'], 1,
    'Span of control refers to the number of employees that a single manager directly supervises. A wide span means many subordinates; a narrow span means few.'),
];

// Lesson 2: The Eight Business Functions
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Eight Business Functions\n\nEvery business, regardless of its size, performs eight key functions. These functions work together to ensure the business runs smoothly.\n\n| Function | Core Purpose |\n|----------|-------------|\n| 1. **General management** | Planning, organising, leading, and controlling the entire business |\n| 2. **Purchasing** | Buying raw materials, stock, and supplies |\n| 3. **Production/Operations** | Converting inputs into finished products or services |\n| 4. **Marketing** | Identifying customer needs and promoting the business |\n| 5. **Financial** | Managing the business\'s money and financial records |\n| 6. **Human resources** | Managing employees — recruitment, training, motivation |\n| 7. **Administration** | Record-keeping, communication, and office management |\n| 8. **Public relations** | Building a positive image and stakeholder relationships |'),
  t(2, '### General Management\n\nGeneral management is the function that co-ordinates all other functions. A general manager performs four key tasks:\n\n1. **Planning** — Setting goals, developing strategies, and deciding on a course of action\n   - Example: The CEO of Woolworths plans the company\'s five-year strategy\n\n2. **Organising** — Arranging resources and tasks to achieve the plan\n   - Example: Assigning departments, allocating budgets, setting up teams\n\n3. **Leading** — Motivating, guiding, and directing employees\n   - Example: A store manager inspiring staff to achieve sales targets\n\n4. **Controlling** — Measuring performance against plans and taking corrective action\n   - Example: Reviewing monthly sales figures and adjusting the marketing budget\n\n**Management levels:**\n- **Top management** (CEO, MD, directors) — strategic decisions\n- **Middle management** (department heads, regional managers) — tactical decisions\n- **Lower management** (supervisors, team leaders) — operational decisions'),
  t(3, '### Purchasing and Production Functions\n\n**Purchasing function:**\nThe purchasing function is responsible for obtaining the inputs the business needs to operate.\n\n**Activities include:**\n- Selecting reliable suppliers\n- Negotiating prices and credit terms\n- Ensuring quality of materials received\n- Managing stock levels (not too much, not too little)\n- Processing purchase orders and payments\n\n**Production/Operations function:**\nThe production function converts inputs (raw materials, labour, capital) into outputs (finished goods or services).\n\n**Activities include:**\n- Planning what to produce and how much\n- Managing the manufacturing or service delivery process\n- Ensuring quality standards are met\n- Maintaining machinery and equipment\n- Managing production schedules\n\n**Example:** At Tiger Brands, the purchasing function buys wheat from farmers, and the production function processes it into bread, biscuits, and flour.'),
  q(4, 'Which business function is responsible for converting raw materials into finished products?',
    ['Purchasing', 'Marketing', 'Production', 'Administration'], 2,
    'The production (or operations) function is responsible for converting inputs like raw materials into finished goods or services.'),
  t(5, '### Marketing and Financial Functions\n\n**Marketing function:**\nMarketing identifies customer needs and communicates the value of the business\'s products.\n\n**Activities include:**\n- Market research (understanding what customers want)\n- Advertising and promotion\n- Pricing decisions\n- Distribution (getting products to customers)\n- Branding and packaging\n\n**Financial function:**\nThe financial function manages all money-related aspects of the business.\n\n**Activities include:**\n- Preparing budgets\n- Managing cash flow (money coming in and going out)\n- Keeping financial records\n- Preparing financial statements (income statement, balance sheet)\n- Arranging finance (loans, overdrafts)\n- Managing accounts payable and receivable\n\n**Example:** Pick n Pay\'s marketing function runs the Smart Shopper loyalty programme to attract and retain customers, while its financial function manages the cash flow from over 2 000 stores.'),
  t(6, '### Human Resources, Administration, and Public Relations\n\n**Human resources (HR) function:**\n- Recruiting and selecting employees\n- Training and development\n- Managing employee relations\n- Administering salaries, leave, and benefits\n- Ensuring compliance with labour laws (LRA, BCEA, EEA)\n\n**Administration function:**\n- Managing office operations and procedures\n- Filing and record-keeping\n- Handling incoming and outgoing communication (calls, emails, post)\n- Scheduling meetings and managing diaries\n- Maintaining office equipment and supplies\n\n**Public relations (PR) function:**\n- Building and maintaining a positive image for the business\n- Communicating with the media\n- Managing crises (e.g., product recalls, negative publicity)\n- Corporate social investment (CSI) programmes\n- Organising events and sponsorships\n\n**Example:** Nedbank\'s PR function manages its "See Money Differently" brand and its green environmental initiatives, building a reputation as a responsible bank.'),
  fb(7, 'The four tasks of general management are planning, ___, leading, and ___.',
    ['organising', 'controlling'],
    'The four management tasks (also called the management process) are planning, organising, leading, and controlling.',
    ['Think about the logical sequence: first you plan, then you arrange, then you guide, then you check.']),
  q(8, 'Which business function handles recruitment, training, and employee relations?',
    ['Administration', 'Public relations', 'General management', 'Human resources'], 3,
    'The human resources (HR) function is responsible for all people-related activities including recruitment, training, employee relations, and compliance with labour legislation.'),
  t(9, '### How Business Functions Work Together\n\nThe eight business functions do not operate in isolation. They are interdependent and must work together for the business to succeed.\n\n**Example: Launching a new product**\n\n1. **Marketing** identifies a gap in the market through research\n2. **General management** decides to proceed and allocates resources\n3. **Financial** prepares a budget for the product launch\n4. **Purchasing** sources raw materials from suppliers\n5. **Production** manufactures the product\n6. **HR** trains staff to sell the new product\n7. **Marketing** advertises and promotes the product\n8. **Administration** processes orders and manages documentation\n9. **PR** creates a media campaign to build awareness\n\n**If one function fails, it affects all the others.** For example, if the purchasing function fails to source quality materials, the production function produces inferior goods, marketing cannot promote a quality product, and sales decline — affecting the financial function.'),
  q(10, 'Why is it important for business functions to work together?',
    ['It reduces the number of employees needed', 'Because each function depends on the others to achieve business goals', 'It allows the business to avoid paying taxes', 'It eliminates the need for management'], 1,
    'Business functions are interdependent — each one depends on the others. If one function fails, it creates a chain reaction that affects the entire business.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 2: Quality and Purchasing (Term 1)
// =============================================================================

blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Concept of Quality\n\nQuality means that a product or service meets or exceeds customer expectations. In business, quality is not just about the final product — it applies to every process in the business.\n\n### What Does Quality Mean?\n\nQuality can be defined in different ways:\n- **Customer perspective:** A product does what it is supposed to do, is reliable, and provides value for money\n- **Producer perspective:** A product meets specifications and standards with minimal defects\n- **Standards perspective:** A product meets SABS (South African Bureau of Standards) or ISO standards\n\n**Why quality matters:**\n- Customers return and recommend the business to others\n- Reduces waste and rework costs\n- Builds a strong brand reputation\n- Gives the business a competitive advantage\n- Reduces customer complaints and returns\n- Ensures compliance with legal standards'),
  t(2, '### Quality Control and Quality Assurance\n\n**Quality Control (QC):**\nQuality control involves inspecting and testing finished products to find and remove defects AFTER production.\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| When? | After production |\n| How? | Inspection, testing, sampling |\n| Focus | Detecting defects |\n| Who? | Quality inspectors |\n\n**Quality Assurance (QA):**\nQuality assurance focuses on preventing defects by managing the production PROCESS.\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| When? | During the entire production process |\n| How? | Setting standards, training workers, monitoring processes |\n| Focus | Preventing defects |\n| Who? | All employees involved in production |\n\n**Key difference:** QC is reactive (finds problems after they happen). QA is proactive (prevents problems before they happen).'),
  q(3, 'What is the KEY difference between quality control and quality assurance?',
    ['QC is more expensive than QA', 'QC finds defects after production; QA prevents defects during production', 'QA is only used in factories', 'QC involves all employees while QA involves only managers'], 1,
    'Quality control (QC) inspects finished products to detect defects after production. Quality assurance (QA) manages the process to prevent defects from occurring in the first place.'),
  t(4, '### The Importance of Quality for Businesses\n\nQuality affects every aspect of a business:\n\n**Impact on customers:**\n- Satisfied customers become repeat buyers\n- Word-of-mouth recommendations bring new customers\n- Poor quality leads to returns, complaints, and lost customers\n\n**Impact on costs:**\n- High quality reduces waste and rework\n- Fewer product returns save money\n- Prevention (QA) is cheaper than correction (QC)\n\n**Impact on reputation:**\n- Consistent quality builds a strong brand\n- One quality failure can damage years of goodwill\n- Social media means quality failures spread quickly\n\n**Impact on competitiveness:**\n- Quality differentiates businesses in crowded markets\n- Customers are willing to pay more for quality\n\n**South African example:** Woolworths built its entire brand around quality. Their food products consistently score higher in quality tests than competitors. This allows Woolworths to charge premium prices because customers trust the quality.'),
  t(5, '### The Purchasing Function in Detail\n\nThe purchasing function ensures the business gets the right inputs, at the right quality, at the right price, at the right time, and from the right supplier.\n\n**The "Five Rights" of Purchasing:**\n\n| Right | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Right quality** | Materials must meet the business\'s specifications |\n| **Right quantity** | Not too much (excess stock costs money) and not too little (stockouts lose sales) |\n| **Right price** | Best value for money without sacrificing quality |\n| **Right time** | Delivered when needed to keep production running |\n| **Right supplier** | Reliable, consistent, and ethical suppliers |\n\n**The purchasing process:**\n1. Identify the need for materials or supplies\n2. Find potential suppliers\n3. Request quotations and compare\n4. Select the best supplier\n5. Place the order\n6. Receive and inspect delivery\n7. Process payment'),
  fb(6, 'The five rights of purchasing are: right quality, right quantity, right ___, right time, and right ___.',
    ['price', 'supplier'],
    'The purchasing function must ensure the right quality, quantity, price, time, and supplier for all inputs.',
    ['Think about getting the best value and finding someone reliable to buy from.']),
  t(7, '### NCA, CPA, and Purchasing Legislation\n\nSeveral South African laws protect businesses and consumers in purchasing transactions:\n\n**National Credit Act (NCA):**\n- Regulates credit agreements (buying on credit, loans)\n- Protects consumers from reckless lending\n- Requires credit providers to assess affordability before granting credit\n- Prevents businesses from exploiting consumers with excessive interest rates\n\n**Consumer Protection Act (CPA):**\n- Protects consumers\' rights when buying goods and services\n- Gives consumers the right to return defective goods within 6 months\n- Requires businesses to provide accurate product information\n- Prohibits unfair marketing and pricing practices\n- Ensures product safety standards are met\n\n**Example:** If a consumer buys a cellphone from a retailer and it stops working within six months through no fault of the consumer, the CPA allows the consumer to return it for a repair, replacement, or refund.'),
  q(8, 'Under the Consumer Protection Act, how long do consumers have to return defective goods?',
    ['30 days', '3 months', '6 months', '1 year'], 2,
    'The Consumer Protection Act gives consumers the right to return defective goods within six months of purchase for a repair, replacement, or refund.'),
  t(9, '### Types of Purchasing\n\n**Centralised purchasing:**\n- One department handles all purchasing for the entire business\n- Advantages: bulk discounts, consistency, specialist buyers\n- Disadvantages: slow, may not suit individual branch needs\n\n**Decentralised purchasing:**\n- Each department or branch handles its own purchasing\n- Advantages: faster, tailored to local needs\n- Disadvantages: higher prices (no bulk discounts), inconsistency\n\n**Just-in-Time (JIT) purchasing:**\n- Materials are ordered to arrive exactly when needed\n- Reduces storage costs and waste\n- Requires very reliable suppliers\n- Risk: any supply disruption stops production\n\n**Example:** Toyota South Africa uses JIT purchasing for its Durban plant. Components arrive from suppliers just hours before they are needed on the assembly line, minimising storage costs but requiring precise coordination.'),
  q(10, 'What is the MAIN advantage of Just-in-Time purchasing?',
    ['Products are always available', 'It reduces storage costs and waste', 'It is the cheapest purchasing method', 'It eliminates the need for suppliers'], 1,
    'JIT purchasing reduces storage costs and minimises waste by ensuring materials arrive exactly when they are needed in the production process.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 3: The Market Environment (Term 1)
// =============================================================================

blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Market Environment\n\nThe market environment consists of external factors that **directly interact** with the business on a regular basis. The business has **limited influence** over these factors — it cannot control them but can take actions to manage its relationships with them.\n\n**Components of the market environment:**\n\n| Component | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Consumers/Customers** | People or businesses that buy the products or services |\n| **Suppliers** | Businesses that provide raw materials, stock, or services |\n| **Competitors** | Other businesses offering similar products to the same market |\n| **Intermediaries** | Agents, wholesalers, and retailers that help distribute products |\n| **Trade unions** | Organisations representing workers\' interests |\n| **Regulators** | Industry bodies and government agencies that set rules |\n\nThe market environment is sometimes called the **task environment** or **operating environment** because it is the environment in which the business carries out its daily activities.'),
  t(2, '### Consumers and Suppliers\n\n**Consumers** are the most important component of the market environment. Without customers, a business cannot survive.\n\n**How businesses interact with consumers:**\n- Market research to understand needs and preferences\n- Product development to meet those needs\n- Competitive pricing to attract purchases\n- Customer service to build loyalty\n- Advertising and promotion to create awareness\n\n**Suppliers** provide the inputs a business needs to operate.\n\n**Factors when choosing suppliers:**\n- Quality of materials supplied\n- Reliability of delivery\n- Price competitiveness\n- Credit terms offered\n- Location (closer suppliers reduce transport costs)\n- Ethical practices (fair labour, environmental compliance)\n\n**Example:** Shoprite works with thousands of local suppliers. Its Supplier Development Programme helps small South African farmers and manufacturers meet Shoprite\'s quality standards, creating jobs and supporting local businesses.'),
  t(3, '### Competitors and Intermediaries\n\n**Competitors** are other businesses that offer similar products or services to the same target market.\n\n**Types of competitors:**\n- **Direct competitors** — sell the same product (e.g., Vodacom and MTN both sell mobile data)\n- **Indirect competitors** — sell substitute products (e.g., Netflix vs going to the cinema)\n\n**Why competition matters:**\n- Forces businesses to improve quality and reduce prices\n- Encourages innovation\n- Gives consumers more choices\n- Drives economic growth\n\n**Intermediaries** help move products from the producer to the final consumer:\n- **Wholesalers** — buy in bulk from producers, sell to retailers\n- **Retailers** — sell directly to consumers\n- **Agents/Brokers** — connect buyers and sellers for commission\n- **Distributors** — handle logistics and delivery\n\n**Example:** Samsung produces smartphones in factories. Distributors transport them to retailers like Vodacom, MTN, iStore, and Takealot, who sell them to consumers.'),
  q(4, 'Vodacom and MTN are examples of which type of competitors?',
    ['Indirect competitors', 'Potential competitors', 'Direct competitors', 'Substitute competitors'], 2,
    'Vodacom and MTN are direct competitors because they both sell the same type of product (mobile services) to the same target market.'),
  t(5, '### Trade Unions and Regulators\n\n**Trade unions** represent the interests of workers and negotiate with employers on issues like wages, working conditions, and job security.\n\n**Major trade union federations in South Africa:**\n- **COSATU** (Congress of South African Trade Unions) — the largest, allied with the ANC\n- **FEDUSA** (Federation of Unions of South Africa) — independent\n- **SAFTU** (South African Federation of Trade Unions) — formed in 2017\n\n**How unions affect businesses:**\n- Collective bargaining increases wages (higher costs)\n- Strikes can disrupt production and cause losses\n- Unions ensure fair treatment of workers\n- Unions can improve workplace safety and conditions\n\n**Regulators** are government agencies and industry bodies that set and enforce rules:\n- **SARS** — Tax compliance\n- **Department of Labour** — Labour law compliance\n- **Competition Commission** — Prevents monopolies and price-fixing\n- **ICASA** — Regulates telecommunications\n- **SABS** — Sets product quality standards'),
  fb(6, 'The market environment has ___ influence by the business, meaning the business can take actions to manage these factors but cannot fully ___ them.',
    ['limited', 'control'],
    'The market environment is external but directly interacts with the business. The business can influence these factors through its strategies but cannot fully control them.',
    ['Think about whether the business can change these factors completely.']),
  t(7, '### The Relationship Between the Business and Its Market\n\n**How the business influences the market environment:**\n\n| Component | How the Business Can Influence |\n|-----------|-------------------------------|\n| Consumers | Quality products, good service, competitive prices, loyalty programmes |\n| Suppliers | Long-term contracts, prompt payment, building partnerships |\n| Competitors | Differentiation, innovation, pricing strategies, advertising |\n| Intermediaries | Exclusive distribution agreements, incentives, support |\n| Trade unions | Fair wages, open communication, good working conditions |\n| Regulators | Compliance, self-regulation, lobbying through industry associations |\n\n**Why the market environment poses a challenge:**\n- Consumer tastes change constantly\n- New competitors can enter at any time\n- Suppliers may increase prices or deliver late\n- Strike action can disrupt operations for weeks\n- Regulations can change unexpectedly'),
  q(8, 'Which government body is responsible for preventing monopolies and price-fixing in South Africa?',
    ['SARS', 'SABS', 'Competition Commission', 'Department of Labour'], 2,
    'The Competition Commission investigates and prevents anti-competitive practices such as monopolies, price-fixing, and market dominance abuse.'),
  t(9, '### Opportunities and Threats in the Market Environment\n\nThe market environment creates both opportunities and threats for businesses:\n\n**Opportunities:**\n- Growing consumer demand in a product category\n- Weak competitors leaving market gaps\n- New distribution channels (e.g., online selling)\n- Favourable supplier terms for long-term partners\n- Changes in regulations opening new markets\n\n**Threats:**\n- New, strong competitors entering the market\n- Changing consumer preferences away from your product\n- Supplier price increases squeezing profit margins\n- Labour unrest and strike action\n- Stricter regulations increasing compliance costs\n\n**Example:** The rise of ride-hailing apps like Uber and Bolt created a major threat for traditional metered taxis in South Africa. Consumers switched to the convenience and safety of app-based transport, forcing the taxi industry to adapt or lose customers.'),
  q(10, 'The rise of Uber and Bolt is an example of which type of market environment challenge for metered taxis?',
    ['A supplier challenge', 'A regulatory challenge', 'A competitor challenge', 'An internal challenge'], 2,
    'Uber and Bolt are competitors to metered taxis, offering a substitute service. This is a market-environment challenge because competition is a component of the market environment.'),
];

