// CHAPTER 4: The Macro Environment (Term 1)
// =============================================================================

// Lesson 1: PESTLE Factors
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Macro Environment\n\nThe macro environment consists of broad external forces that affect **all businesses** in the economy. Individual businesses have **no direct control** over these factors and must adapt to them.\n\nThe macro environment can be analysed using the **PESTLE framework:**\n\n| Letter | Factor | Examples |\n|--------|--------|----------|\n| **P** | Political | Government policies, stability, trade agreements |\n| **E** | Economic | Inflation, interest rates, exchange rates, unemployment |\n| **S** | Social | Demographics, culture, health issues, crime |\n| **T** | Technological | New inventions, automation, internet, AI |\n| **L** | Legal | Labour laws, consumer protection, tax laws |\n| **E** | Ecological/Environmental | Climate change, pollution, water scarcity |\n\nEach PESTLE factor can create both **opportunities** and **threats** for businesses.'),
  t(2, '### Political Factors\n\nPolitical factors relate to government actions and political conditions that affect businesses.\n\n**Key political factors in South Africa:**\n\n- **Government policies** — BBBEE (Broad-Based Black Economic Empowerment) encourages transformation but adds compliance requirements\n- **Political stability** — Political uncertainty can scare away investors and reduce economic growth\n- **Trade agreements** — AfCFTA (African Continental Free Trade Area) opens African markets to SA businesses\n- **Service delivery** — Municipal failures (water, electricity, roads) disrupt business operations\n- **Load shedding** — Energy crisis caused by Eskom\'s management failures forces businesses to spend on generators and solar\n\n**Impact on business:**\n- Businesses must comply with BBBEE requirements to win government contracts\n- Political instability (like the July 2021 unrest in KZN and Gauteng) can cause billions of rands in damage\n- Good trade policies can open new export markets\n\n**Example:** After the July 2021 unrest, over 200 shopping centres and 3 000 retail stores were damaged or destroyed, costing the economy an estimated R50 billion.'),
  t(3, '### Economic Factors\n\nEconomic factors directly affect how much money consumers and businesses have.\n\n**Key economic factors:**\n\n| Factor | Description | Impact on Business |\n|--------|-------------|-------------------|\n| **Inflation** | General increase in prices over time | Increases production costs; reduces consumer purchasing power |\n| **Interest rates** | Cost of borrowing money | Higher rates = more expensive loans, less consumer spending on credit |\n| **Exchange rates** | Value of the rand against other currencies | Weak rand = expensive imports, cheaper exports |\n| **Unemployment** | Percentage of people who want work but cannot find it | Reduces consumer spending; increases the labour supply |\n| **GDP growth** | Rate at which the economy is growing | Low growth = fewer opportunities for businesses |\n\n**South African context:**\n- SA\'s unemployment rate is over 30% (one of the highest in the world)\n- Inflation has averaged around 5-7% in recent years\n- The rand has weakened significantly against the dollar over the past decade\n- GDP growth has been very slow (below 2%) due to structural challenges'),
  q(4, 'How does a WEAK rand affect a South African business that imports raw materials?',
    ['It makes imports cheaper', 'It has no effect on imports', 'It makes imports more expensive', 'It eliminates the need for imports'], 2,
    'A weak rand means more rands are needed to buy the same amount of foreign currency, making imported raw materials and goods more expensive for South African businesses.'),
  t(5, '### Social and Technological Factors\n\n**Social factors** relate to the characteristics and behaviour of the population:\n\n- **Demographics** — SA has a young population (median age around 28 years)\n- **HIV/AIDS** — Affects workforce productivity and increases healthcare costs\n- **Crime** — High crime rates increase security costs for businesses\n- **Education levels** — Skills shortages in certain sectors\n- **Cultural diversity** — 11 official languages, diverse consumer preferences\n- **Urbanisation** — More people moving to cities, creating larger urban markets\n\n**Technological factors** relate to new discoveries and innovations:\n\n- **E-commerce** — Online shopping growing rapidly (Takealot, Checkers Sixty60, Shein)\n- **Mobile technology** — Most South Africans access the internet via smartphones\n- **Social media** — Businesses must have a social media presence\n- **Automation** — Machines and AI replacing some manual jobs\n- **Digital payments** — SnapScan, Zapper, and digital wallets becoming common\n\n**Example:** Checkers launched the Sixty60 app, using technology to deliver groceries in 60 minutes, transforming how South Africans shop for food.'),
  fb(6, 'PESTLE stands for Political, ___, Social, Technological, Legal, and ___.',
    ['Economic', 'Ecological'],
    'PESTLE is a framework for analysing the macro environment. It covers Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Legal, and Ecological factors.',
    ['Think about the factors related to money and to the natural world.']),
  t(7, '### Legal and Ecological Factors\n\n**Legal factors** are laws and regulations that businesses must comply with:\n\n| Law | Purpose |\n|-----|----------|\n| **Labour Relations Act (LRA)** | Governs the relationship between employers, employees, and unions |\n| **Basic Conditions of Employment Act (BCEA)** | Sets minimum working conditions (hours, leave, overtime) |\n| **Employment Equity Act (EEA)** | Promotes equal opportunity and eliminates workplace discrimination |\n| **Consumer Protection Act (CPA)** | Protects consumers\' rights |\n| **POPIA** | Protects personal information/data |\n| **Companies Act** | Regulates the formation and management of companies |\n| **BBBEE Act** | Promotes economic transformation |\n\n**Ecological/Environmental factors:**\n- Water scarcity (e.g., Cape Town Day Zero crisis in 2018)\n- Climate change affecting agriculture\n- Pressure to reduce carbon emissions\n- Strict environmental impact assessments for new developments\n- Growing demand for sustainable and eco-friendly products\n\n**Example:** The 2018 Cape Town water crisis forced businesses to install water-saving devices, recycle water, and find alternative sources, significantly increasing operating costs.'),
  q(8, 'Which South African law protects personal information and data?',
    ['Consumer Protection Act', 'Labour Relations Act', 'POPIA', 'Companies Act'], 2,
    'POPIA (Protection of Personal Information Act) regulates how businesses collect, store, process, and share personal information. It protects individuals\' right to privacy.'),
  t(9, '### Challenges of the Macro Environment\n\nBecause businesses cannot control the macro environment, they must develop strategies to adapt:\n\n| Challenge | Adaptation Strategy |\n|-----------|--------------------|\n| Load shedding | Invest in solar panels, generators, battery backup, UPS systems |\n| High inflation | Reduce costs, negotiate with suppliers, increase efficiency |\n| Weak rand | Source locally instead of importing, export to earn foreign currency |\n| High crime | Invest in security systems, insurance, and staff training |\n| Skills shortage | Partner with SETAs for training, offer bursaries, upskill existing staff |\n| Water scarcity | Install water-saving devices, recycle water, harvest rainwater |\n| Changing technology | Embrace digital transformation, train staff in new technology |\n\n**Example:** Shoprite Group invested over R3 billion in solar panels and alternative energy to reduce their dependence on Eskom, turning a macro-environment threat into a competitive advantage by lowering long-term energy costs.'),
  q(10, 'A business has NO control over which type of environment?',
    ['Micro environment', 'Market environment', 'Macro environment', 'All environments equally'], 2,
    'The macro environment consists of broad external forces (PESTLE factors) that no individual business can control. Businesses must adapt to these factors.'),
];

// Lesson 2: Interrelationship Between Environments
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Interrelationship Between Business Environments\n\nThe three business environments do not exist separately — they are interconnected and constantly influence each other. Changes in one environment often trigger changes in the others.\n\n### How the Environments Are Connected\n\n| Connection | Example |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Macro affects Market | A recession (economic/macro) reduces consumer spending (market) |\n| Macro affects Micro | New labour laws (legal/macro) require HR policy changes (micro) |\n| Market affects Micro | A new competitor (market) forces the business to improve quality (micro) |\n| Micro affects Market | A product innovation (micro) attracts more customers (market) |\n| Market affects Macro | Large-scale retrenchments across an industry (market) increase unemployment (macro) |\n\nA successful business monitors ALL three environments and responds proactively rather than waiting for problems to arise.'),
  t(2, '### The Business and the Micro Environment\n\nThe micro environment is the foundation of the business. If the internal factors are strong, the business is better equipped to handle market and macro challenges.\n\n**Strong micro environment:**\n- Skilled, motivated employees handle customer complaints effectively (improves market relationships)\n- Good financial management creates reserves for economic downturns (adapts to macro challenges)\n- Innovation and creativity lead to new products (creates market opportunities)\n- Strong leadership provides direction during uncertain times\n\n**Weak micro environment:**\n- Poor management leads to wrong decisions about the market\n- Lack of financial reserves means the business cannot survive economic shocks\n- Low employee morale leads to poor customer service\n- Outdated technology means the business cannot compete\n\n**Example:** During COVID-19, businesses with strong micro environments (good management, financial reserves, adaptable culture) survived, while businesses with weak foundations were more likely to close.'),
  q(3, 'A new government law requiring businesses to pay higher minimum wages is an example of which environment affecting which?',
    ['Micro affecting market', 'Market affecting micro', 'Macro affecting micro', 'Micro affecting macro'], 2,
    'A new government law is a legal/macro-environment factor. When it forces the business to change its internal HR policies and budgets, the macro environment is affecting the micro environment.'),
  t(4, '### The Relationship Between the Business and the Market\n\nThe market environment is where the business interacts with external stakeholders on a daily basis.\n\n**How the business influences the market:**\n- Quality products attract loyal customers\n- Fair treatment of suppliers builds strong partnerships\n- Competitive strategies challenge competitors\n- Good union relations prevent strikes\n\n**How the market influences the business:**\n- Consumer preferences dictate what products to develop\n- Competitor actions force strategic responses\n- Supplier reliability affects production schedules\n- Union demands affect wage budgets\n\n**Example of the two-way relationship:**\nWhen Checkers launched Sixty60, it influenced the market by changing consumer expectations for speed. This in turn forced competitors (Pick n Pay, Woolworths) to launch their own delivery services. The consumers then benefited from more choices and better service.'),
  t(5, '### The Relationship Between the Market and the Macro Environment\n\nThe macro environment creates the conditions in which the market operates.\n\n**How macro affects the market:**\n- A recession (economic) reduces consumer spending power\n- New technology enables new competitors to enter the market\n- Political instability scares away foreign suppliers and investors\n- Legal changes (like POPIA) change how businesses interact with customers\n\n**How the market can influence the macro:**\n- Widespread retrenchments increase national unemployment\n- Industry lobbying can influence government policy\n- Consumer movements can drive environmental legislation\n- Industry innovation can drive technological advancement\n\n**Example:** When South African mobile networks reduced data prices (market-level competition), more people gained internet access (social/macro change), which enabled the growth of e-commerce and digital services across the economy.'),
  fb(6, 'The three business environments are the ___ (internal), the ___ (directly interacting), and the macro (broad external forces).',
    ['micro', 'market'],
    'The three layers are the micro environment (internal), the market environment (directly interacting stakeholders), and the macro environment (broad external forces).',
    ['Think about the three layers from inside to outside.']),
  q(7, 'If a business improves its product quality (micro) and this attracts more customers (market), this shows that:',
    ['The macro environment controls everything', 'The environments are independent of each other', 'Changes in one environment can influence other environments', 'Only the micro environment matters'], 2,
    'This example shows that the three business environments are interconnected. An improvement in the micro environment (product quality) positively affects the market environment (customer attraction).'),
  t(8, '### Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, and Threats (SWOT)\n\nSWOT analysis is a tool that connects the internal and external environments:\n\n| | Positive | Negative |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Internal** (micro) | **Strengths** | **Weaknesses** |\n| **External** (market + macro) | **Opportunities** | **Threats** |\n\n**Example SWOT: A small coffee shop in Sandton**\n\n| Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|-----------|------------|\n| Unique, locally roasted coffee blends | Small with only 15 seats |\n| Friendly, well-trained staff | No online ordering system |\n| Strong social media presence | Limited financial reserves |\n\n| Opportunities | Threats |\n|---------------|----------|\n| Growing trend for artisanal coffee | New Starbucks opening nearby |\n| Uber Eats and Mr D deliveries | Load shedding affecting coffee machines |\n| Office workers in Sandton CBD | Rising milk and coffee bean prices |\n\nA SWOT analysis helps the business match its strengths to opportunities and prepare for threats.'),
  q(9, 'In a SWOT analysis, "rising rent prices in Sandton" would be classified as a:',
    ['Strength', 'Weakness', 'Opportunity', 'Threat'], 3,
    'Rising rent is an external factor that could harm the business, making it a threat. Threats come from the market or macro environment and are outside the business\'s control.'),
  t(10, '### Summary: The Three Business Environments\n\n| Feature | Micro | Market | Macro |\n|---------|-------|--------|-------|\n| **Location** | Internal | Directly outside | Broad external |\n| **Control** | Full control | Limited influence | No control |\n| **Components** | Vision, mission, culture, resources, functions, structure | Consumers, suppliers, competitors, intermediaries, unions, regulators | PESTLE factors |\n| **Response** | Change and improve | Manage and influence | Monitor and adapt |\n| **Example** | Improve staff training | Build customer loyalty programme | Install solar panels for load shedding |\n\n**Key insight:** The most successful businesses are those that build strong micro environments, actively manage their market relationships, and proactively adapt to macro-environment changes rather than simply reacting when problems arise.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 5: Business Sectors (Term 2)
// =============================================================================

blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Business Sectors\n\nThe economy is divided into different sectors based on the type of economic activity businesses perform. Understanding sectors helps us see how the economy works and how businesses depend on each other.\n\n### The Three Main Sectors\n\n| Sector | Activity | Examples |\n|--------|----------|----------|\n| **Primary sector** | Extraction of raw materials from nature | Mining, farming, fishing, forestry |\n| **Secondary sector** | Processing and manufacturing raw materials into finished goods | Factories, construction, food processing |\n| **Tertiary sector** | Providing services | Banking, retail, transport, healthcare |\n\nEach sector plays an essential role in the economy, and the sectors are interdependent — they depend on each other to function.'),
  t(2, '### The Primary Sector\n\nThe primary sector involves extracting natural resources directly from the earth or nature.\n\n**Industries in the primary sector:**\n- **Mining** — Gold, platinum, coal, diamonds, iron ore, manganese, chrome\n- **Agriculture** — Maize, wheat, sugarcane, citrus fruits, wine grapes, livestock\n- **Fishing** — Hake, pilchards, tuna, squid, rock lobster\n- **Forestry** — Timber, wood pulp for paper production\n\n**Importance to South Africa:**\n- Mining contributed approximately 8% of GDP and employed over 450 000 workers\n- Agriculture employs about 900 000 people, mainly in rural areas\n- SA is one of the world\'s largest producers of platinum, gold, and chrome\n- Agriculture supports food security for the nation\n\n**Example:** Anglo American Platinum mines platinum group metals in Limpopo and North West provinces. This raw material is exported or used in the automotive industry for catalytic converters.'),
  t(3, '### The Secondary Sector\n\nThe secondary sector takes raw materials from the primary sector and transforms them into finished or semi-finished products.\n\n**Industries in the secondary sector:**\n- **Manufacturing** — Food processing, vehicle assembly, textiles, chemicals, electronics\n- **Construction** — Building roads, bridges, houses, shopping centres\n- **Electricity, gas, and water** — Eskom power generation, municipal water treatment\n\n**Importance to South Africa:**\n- Manufacturing contributes about 13% of GDP\n- The automotive industry is a major export earner (BMW, Toyota, Mercedes-Benz, VW all have SA factories)\n- Food processing is the largest manufacturing sub-sector (Tiger Brands, Pioneer Foods)\n- Construction provides employment for many low-skilled workers\n\n**Example:** SAPPI takes raw timber from forestry plantations (primary sector) and processes it into paper and dissolving wood pulp (secondary sector) at its mills in Mpumalanga and KwaZulu-Natal.'),
  q(4, 'A factory that assembles motor vehicles belongs to which sector?',
    ['Primary sector', 'Secondary sector', 'Tertiary sector', 'Informal sector'], 1,
    'Vehicle assembly is a manufacturing activity that transforms components into finished products. Manufacturing belongs to the secondary sector.'),
  t(5, '### The Tertiary Sector\n\nThe tertiary sector provides services rather than physical products. It is the **largest sector** in South Africa, contributing over 65% of GDP.\n\n**Industries in the tertiary sector:**\n- **Retail and wholesale** — Shoprite, Pick n Pay, Makro\n- **Financial services** — Banks, insurance companies, investment firms (FNB, Discovery, Sanlam)\n- **Transport and logistics** — Airlines, freight companies, Uber, Bolt\n- **Telecommunications** — Vodacom, MTN, Telkom, Rain\n- **Tourism and hospitality** — Hotels, restaurants, game lodges, tour operators\n- **Healthcare** — Private hospitals, clinics, pharmacies\n- **Education** — Schools, universities, training institutions\n- **Professional services** — Lawyers, accountants, consultants\n\n**Importance to South Africa:**\n- Employs the most people in the economy\n- Financial services and tourism are major contributors\n- Retail provides essential goods access to all communities\n\n**Example:** Discovery provides health insurance, life insurance, and banking services to millions of South Africans, making it one of the largest service businesses in the country.'),
  fb(6, 'The primary sector ___ raw materials, the secondary sector ___ them into finished goods, and the tertiary sector provides ___.',
    ['extracts', 'processes', 'services'],
    'Primary = extraction from nature, Secondary = processing/manufacturing, Tertiary = providing services.',
    ['Think about what each sector DOES: take from nature, make products, help people.']),
  t(7, '### Links Between Sectors (The Value Chain)\n\nThe three sectors are connected through value chains. A product typically passes through all three sectors before reaching the consumer.\n\n**Example 1: Bread**\n1. **Primary:** A farmer in the Free State grows wheat\n2. **Secondary:** A bakery (e.g., Albany) mills the wheat into flour and bakes bread\n3. **Tertiary:** Pick n Pay sells the bread to consumers\n\n**Example 2: Petrol**\n1. **Primary:** Oil is extracted from offshore reserves or imported\n2. **Secondary:** Sasol or Enref refines the crude oil into petrol and diesel\n3. **Tertiary:** Engen, Shell, or BP service stations sell petrol to motorists\n\n**Example 3: Furniture**\n1. **Primary:** Trees are harvested from commercial forests in Mpumalanga\n2. **Secondary:** A furniture factory turns the wood into tables, chairs, and beds\n3. **Tertiary:** Builders Warehouse or @Home sells the furniture to consumers\n\nThis interdependence means a disruption in one sector affects the others. If drought damages wheat crops (primary), flour prices rise (secondary), and bread becomes more expensive (tertiary).'),
  q(8, 'Which of the following correctly shows the value chain from primary to secondary to tertiary?',
    ['Bank lends money, factory makes goods, farmer grows crops', 'Farmer grows sugarcane, factory processes sugar, supermarket sells sugar', 'Supermarket sells goods, factory makes goods, miner extracts ore', 'All activities happen simultaneously'], 1,
    'The correct value chain flows from primary (farmer grows sugarcane) to secondary (factory processes it into sugar) to tertiary (supermarket sells sugar to consumers).'),
  t(9, '### The Formal and Informal Sectors\n\nBusinesses can also be classified as formal or informal:\n\n| Feature | Formal Sector | Informal Sector |\n|---------|--------------|----------------|\n| **Registration** | Registered with CIPC and SARS | Not formally registered |\n| **Tax** | Pays income tax, VAT, UIF | Generally does not pay tax |\n| **Labour laws** | Must comply with LRA, BCEA | Often does not comply |\n| **Records** | Keeps formal financial records | Cash-based, limited records |\n| **Examples** | Shoprite, FNB, Toyota | Spaza shops, street vendors, informal traders |\n\n**The informal sector in South Africa:**\n- Provides income for an estimated 2.5 million people\n- Important for communities with limited formal employment\n- Includes street vendors, spaza shops, minibus taxis, hairdressers, car washers\n- Often the first step for entrepreneurs before formalising\n\n**Public vs Private sector:**\n- **Public sector** — owned and run by the government (SAPS, public hospitals, Eskom, Transnet)\n- **Private sector** — owned and run by private individuals or companies (Shoprite, Discovery, MTN)'),
  q(10, 'Which of the following is an example of a business in the informal sector?',
    ['Shoprite', 'Standard Bank', 'A street vendor selling fruit', 'Toyota'], 2,
    'A street vendor is typically not formally registered with CIPC or SARS, operates on a cash basis, and may not comply with formal business regulations — making it part of the informal sector.'),
];

