// CHAPTER 6: Contemporary Socio-Economic Issues (Term 2)
// =============================================================================

blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Contemporary Socio-Economic Issues\n\nSocio-economic issues are social and economic problems that affect businesses, workers, and communities. These challenges create risks for businesses but can also inspire positive action.\n\n### Key Socio-Economic Issues in South Africa\n\n| Issue | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Poverty** | Millions of South Africans live below the poverty line |\n| **Inequality** | SA has one of the highest Gini coefficients (income gap) in the world |\n| **Unemployment** | Over 30% of the labour force cannot find work |\n| **HIV/AIDS** | Affects workforce productivity and increases business costs |\n| **Crime** | High levels of crime increase security costs for businesses |\n| **Gambling** | Social problems affecting families and employee productivity |\n| **Counterfeiting and piracy** | Illegal copies of products reduce legitimate business revenue |\n| **Substance abuse** | Drugs and alcohol affect employee productivity and safety |\n\nThese issues do not exist in isolation — they are interconnected. Poverty leads to crime, crime discourages investment, less investment means fewer jobs, and fewer jobs increase poverty.'),
  t(2, '### Impact of Socio-Economic Issues on Businesses\n\n**Poverty and inequality:**\n- Reduced consumer purchasing power (fewer sales)\n- Smaller domestic market for premium products\n- Higher demand for affordable, basic goods\n- Security risks from desperation-driven crime\n\n**Unemployment:**\n- Large pool of available workers (advantage for businesses)\n- But also means less consumer spending (disadvantage)\n- Creates social instability that can lead to unrest\n- Young people are disproportionately affected (youth unemployment over 60%)\n\n**HIV/AIDS:**\n- Increased absenteeism due to illness\n- Loss of skilled workers\n- Higher medical costs and insurance premiums\n- Need for workplace wellness programmes\n- Training costs when experienced workers become ill\n\n**Example:** SABMiller (now AB InBev) introduced one of South Africa\'s first comprehensive workplace HIV/AIDS programmes, providing free testing, counselling, and antiretroviral treatment to employees. This reduced absenteeism and improved productivity.'),
  q(3, 'How does high unemployment NEGATIVELY affect businesses?',
    ['It increases the supply of workers', 'It reduces consumer spending power', 'It makes recruitment easier', 'It reduces wage demands'], 1,
    'High unemployment means fewer people have income to spend, which reduces consumer spending power and leads to lower sales for businesses.'),
  t(4, '### The Impact of Crime on Businesses\n\nSouth Africa has high levels of crime, which significantly affects businesses:\n\n**Direct costs of crime:**\n- Theft and burglary losses (stock, equipment, cash)\n- Armed robberies (especially cash-in-transit)\n- Cybercrime (data theft, online fraud)\n- Vandalism and property damage\n\n**Indirect costs of crime:**\n- Security costs (guards, cameras, alarm systems, electric fencing)\n- Higher insurance premiums\n- Lost business hours due to security incidents\n- Difficulty attracting foreign investment\n- Employee fear and reduced morale\n- Tourism losses due to safety concerns\n\n**How businesses respond:**\n- Install CCTV cameras and alarm systems\n- Hire private security companies\n- Implement strict access control measures\n- Use technology (GPS tracking, cashless payments)\n- Join community safety initiatives\n\n**Example:** According to Business Against Crime South Africa, businesses spend approximately R50 billion per year on private security, making SA\'s private security industry one of the largest in the world.'),
  t(5, '### Counterfeiting, Piracy, and Gambling\n\n**Counterfeiting** is producing fake copies of branded products:\n- Fake clothing, shoes, electronics, medicines, car parts\n- Poses safety risks (fake medicines can kill, fake car parts can fail)\n- Costs SA businesses billions in lost revenue\n- Example: Fake branded clothing sold at some markets costs the SA fashion industry over R7 billion per year\n\n**Piracy** is the illegal copying of copyrighted material:\n- Music, movies, software, textbooks\n- Reduces revenue for creators and publishers\n- Discourages investment in new creative content\n- Digital piracy has grown with internet access\n\n**Gambling:**\n- Can lead to addiction, financial ruin, and family breakdown\n- Affects employee productivity and reliability\n- Online gambling is growing and harder to regulate\n- The National Gambling Act regulates the industry in SA\n\n**Impact on the economy:**\n- Counterfeiting costs SA an estimated R362 billion per year\n- Results in job losses in legitimate industries\n- Government loses tax revenue from informal/illegal trade'),
  fb(6, 'Socio-economic issues such as poverty, unemployment, and ___ are interconnected and affect both businesses and communities in South Africa.',
    ['crime'],
    'Poverty, unemployment, and crime are three of the major interconnected socio-economic issues in South Africa that create challenges for businesses.',
    ['Think about a social problem that directly increases business security costs.']),
  t(7, '### How Businesses Can Help Address Socio-Economic Issues\n\nBusinesses have a responsibility and an opportunity to help address socio-economic challenges:\n\n| Action | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Job creation** | Hiring local workers, supporting SMMEs in supply chains |\n| **Skills development** | Training programmes, learnerships, apprenticeships, bursaries |\n| **Community investment** | CSI (Corporate Social Investment) programmes |\n| **Employee wellness** | HIV/AIDS programmes, counselling, health screening |\n| **Ethical practices** | Paying fair wages, complying with BBBEE, being transparent |\n| **Education support** | Building schools, providing learning materials, funding scholarships |\n| **Environmental action** | Reducing pollution, conserving water, using renewable energy |\n\n**Example:** Woolworths\' MySchool MyVillage MyPlanet programme allows customers to support schools and community organisations every time they shop, contributing millions of rands annually to education and community development.'),
  q(8, 'What does CSI stand for in a business context?',
    ['Company Stakeholder Investment', 'Corporate Social Investment', 'Consumer Service Initiative', 'Commercial Sustainability Index'], 1,
    'CSI stands for Corporate Social Investment — programmes through which businesses contribute to the social development of communities.'),
  t(9, '### Inclusivity in the Workplace\n\n**Inclusivity** means ensuring that all people, regardless of their background, feel welcome, valued, and able to participate fully in the workplace.\n\n**Why inclusivity matters in South Africa:**\n- Our history of apartheid created deep inequalities that still exist\n- The Constitution (Section 9) guarantees equality and prohibits unfair discrimination\n- The Employment Equity Act promotes equal opportunity\n- BBBEE encourages businesses to transform\n\n**Forms of workplace inclusivity:**\n- Employing people with disabilities\n- Ensuring gender equality in management\n- Respecting cultural and religious diversity\n- Providing equal opportunities regardless of race or ethnicity\n- Accommodating different languages in the workplace\n- Supporting employees living with HIV/AIDS without discrimination\n\n**Benefits of an inclusive workplace:**\n- Diverse perspectives lead to better decision-making\n- Attracts and retains talented employees\n- Improves the business\'s reputation\n- Compliance with legislation (EEA, BBBEE)\n- Reflects the diversity of the South African consumer market'),
  q(10, 'Which South African law promotes equal opportunity and prohibits unfair discrimination in the workplace?',
    ['Labour Relations Act', 'Consumer Protection Act', 'Employment Equity Act', 'Companies Act'], 2,
    'The Employment Equity Act (EEA) promotes equal opportunity, eliminates unfair discrimination, and requires designated employers to implement affirmative action measures.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 7: Social Responsibility (Term 2)
// =============================================================================

blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Social Responsibility\n\n### What Is Social Responsibility?\n\nSocial responsibility is the obligation of a business to act in ways that benefit society, not just its shareholders. A socially responsible business considers the impact of its decisions on all stakeholders — employees, customers, communities, and the environment.\n\n**Social responsibility includes:**\n- Treating employees fairly\n- Producing safe, quality products\n- Protecting the environment\n- Contributing to community development\n- Being honest and transparent\n- Paying taxes and complying with laws\n\n**The term "corporate social responsibility" (CSR)** specifically refers to a company\'s voluntary commitment to social and environmental well-being beyond what the law requires.'),
  t(2, '### Corporate Social Investment (CSI)\n\nCSI is a key part of social responsibility. It involves businesses voluntarily investing time, money, and resources into community projects and social causes.\n\n**Common areas of CSI in South Africa:**\n\n| Area | Examples |\n|------|----------|\n| **Education** | Building schools, funding bursaries, donating computers |\n| **Health** | HIV/AIDS programmes, mobile clinics, health awareness campaigns |\n| **Environment** | Tree planting, river clean-ups, recycling programmes |\n| **Sports development** | Sponsoring grassroots sports, building sports facilities |\n| **Arts and culture** | Supporting museums, music festivals, art programmes |\n| **Food security** | Feeding schemes, community gardens, food banks |\n\n**Examples of CSI in South Africa:**\n- **Old Mutual** — Supports education through scholarships and school programmes\n- **Nedbank** — Green initiatives and environmental conservation projects\n- **Shoprite** — Donates food through its "Act for Change" programme to hundreds of charities\n- **MTN** — Foundation supports education, health, and economic empowerment projects'),
  q(3, 'What is the difference between CSR and CSI?',
    ['They are exactly the same thing', 'CSR is the broad commitment to responsible behaviour; CSI is the specific investment in community projects', 'CSI is mandatory while CSR is voluntary', 'CSR only applies to large companies'], 1,
    'CSR (Corporate Social Responsibility) is the overall commitment to ethical and responsible business practices. CSI (Corporate Social Investment) is a specific component — the actual investment of resources into community and social projects.'),
  t(4, '### Why Businesses Should Be Socially Responsible\n\n**Benefits of social responsibility:**\n\n| Benefit | Explanation |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Enhanced reputation** | Customers prefer to support responsible businesses |\n| **Customer loyalty** | Consumers choose brands aligned with their values |\n| **Employee attraction and retention** | Workers want to work for ethical companies |\n| **Investor confidence** | Responsible businesses are seen as lower risk |\n| **Competitive advantage** | Differentiation from competitors |\n| **Reduced legal problems** | Proactive compliance reduces risk of fines |\n| **Community support** | Good relationships with local communities reduce conflict |\n| **Long-term sustainability** | Businesses that damage society eventually damage themselves |\n\n**The business case:** Social responsibility is not just the "right thing to do" — it is good business. Research consistently shows that socially responsible companies outperform irresponsible ones over the long term.'),
  t(5, '### Social Responsibility Towards Stakeholders\n\nA business has responsibilities towards different stakeholder groups:\n\n**Towards employees:**\n- Fair wages and safe working conditions\n- Training and development opportunities\n- Non-discrimination and equal opportunity\n- Work-life balance\n\n**Towards customers:**\n- Safe, quality products\n- Honest advertising\n- Fair pricing\n- Protection of personal information (POPIA compliance)\n\n**Towards the community:**\n- Job creation and local procurement\n- CSI programmes\n- Minimising noise, traffic, and pollution\n- Supporting local initiatives\n\n**Towards the environment:**\n- Reducing carbon emissions\n- Responsible waste management\n- Sustainable use of natural resources\n- Compliance with environmental laws'),
  fb(6, 'A business has social responsibilities towards its employees, customers, ___, and the ___.',
    ['community', 'environment'],
    'The four main stakeholder groups towards which a business has social responsibilities are employees, customers, community, and the environment.',
    ['Think about the groups beyond just the people inside the business and its direct buyers.']),
  t(7, '### BBBEE and Social Responsibility\n\nBroad-Based Black Economic Empowerment (BBBEE) is a South African government programme that aims to address the economic inequalities created by apartheid.\n\n**BBBEE scorecard elements:**\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Ownership** | Black ownership of the business |\n| **Management control** | Black representation in management and board positions |\n| **Skills development** | Training and developing black employees |\n| **Enterprise and supplier development** | Supporting black-owned SMMEs and preferring black suppliers |\n| **Socio-economic development** | CSI contributions to black communities |\n\n**Why BBBEE matters for business:**\n- Higher BBBEE rating = better chance of winning government tenders\n- Many large companies require suppliers to have good BBBEE scores\n- Contributes to transformation and economic inclusivity\n- Can access government incentives and support\n\n**BBBEE levels range from Level 1 (highest) to Level 8 (lowest), plus non-compliant.**'),
  q(8, 'What is the primary purpose of the BBBEE programme?',
    ['To increase business profits', 'To reduce competition', 'To address economic inequalities created by apartheid', 'To replace white-owned businesses'], 2,
    'BBBEE aims to address the economic inequalities created by apartheid by promoting the meaningful participation of black South Africans in the economy through ownership, management, skills development, and enterprise development.'),
  t(9, '### Environmental Responsibility\n\nBusinesses have a growing responsibility to protect the environment:\n\n**Environmental challenges in South Africa:**\n- Water scarcity and pollution\n- Air pollution from mining and industry\n- Waste management and illegal dumping\n- Climate change and extreme weather events\n- Loss of biodiversity\n\n**How businesses can be environmentally responsible:**\n- Install solar panels and use renewable energy\n- Reduce, reuse, and recycle waste\n- Use water-efficient processes\n- Reduce packaging and use eco-friendly materials\n- Conduct environmental impact assessments before new projects\n- Report on environmental performance (sustainability reporting)\n- Offset carbon emissions through tree planting or carbon credits\n\n**Example:** Woolworths\' Good Business Journey commits to zero waste to landfill, sustainably sourcing all key commodities, and reducing water and energy use across all operations. They report progress annually in their sustainability report.'),
  q(10, 'Which of the following is NOT an example of environmental responsibility by a business?',
    ['Installing solar panels', 'Recycling waste materials', 'Dumping waste in a river to save costs', 'Reducing packaging'], 2,
    'Dumping waste in a river is illegal and environmentally irresponsible. It harms the environment, communities, and is punishable under South African environmental legislation.'),
];

