// CHAPTER 8: Forms of Ownership (Term 2)
// =============================================================================

// Lesson 1: Sole Trader and Partnership
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Forms of Business Ownership\n\nChoosing the right form of ownership is one of the most important decisions an entrepreneur makes. The form of ownership affects who controls the business, who is liable for its debts, how profits are shared, and how much capital can be raised.\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n| Concept | Definition |\n|---------|------------|\n| **Legal personality** | Whether the business is a separate legal entity from its owner(s) |\n| **Limited liability** | Owner\'s personal assets are protected from business debts |\n| **Unlimited liability** | Owner\'s personal assets can be used to pay business debts |\n| **Continuity** | Whether the business continues to exist if the owner dies or leaves |\n| **Capital** | Money and resources available to start and run the business |\n\n**Why the form of ownership matters:**\n- Determines who is responsible for debts\n- Affects how easy it is to raise money\n- Determines tax implications\n- Affects the level of control the owner has\n- Influences the business\'s credibility'),
  t(2, '### Sole Proprietor (Sole Trader)\n\nA sole proprietor is a business owned and managed by **one person**. It is the simplest and most common form of ownership in South Africa.\n\n**Characteristics:**\n- The owner and the business are legally the SAME entity (no separate legal personality)\n- The owner makes all decisions and keeps all profits\n- **Unlimited liability** — the owner is personally responsible for ALL business debts\n- No legal formalities required to start (but must register with SARS for tax)\n- The business has no continuity — it dies with the owner\n\n**Examples:** Many spaza shops, hairdressers, plumbers, photographers, tutors, and freelancers operate as sole proprietors.\n\n**Example:** Thabo starts a car wash business in his yard. He invests R5 000 of his own savings, buys equipment, and operates alone. All profits are his, but if someone gets injured on his property, Thabo is personally responsible for all costs.'),
  t(3, '### Advantages and Disadvantages of a Sole Proprietor\n\n| Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|------------|---------------|\n| Easy and cheap to start | **Unlimited liability** (personal assets at risk) |\n| Owner keeps ALL profits | Limited capital (only own savings and small loans) |\n| Owner makes ALL decisions (fast) | Limited skills (owner must do everything) |\n| Minimal legal requirements | **No continuity** (business ends when owner dies) |\n| Privacy (no published financial statements) | Banks see it as risky (difficult to get loans) |\n| Owner is directly motivated | Heavy workload, long hours, no leave cover |\n| Flexible — can adapt quickly to changes | Cannot easily grow or expand |\n\n**Who should choose this form?**\n- Someone starting a small business with limited capital\n- A person who wants full control and independence\n- Businesses with low financial risk\n- Someone testing a business idea before formalising'),
  q(4, 'What does "unlimited liability" mean for a sole proprietor?',
    ['The business has unlimited capital', 'The owner is personally responsible for all business debts', 'The owner can borrow unlimited amounts', 'The business can have unlimited employees'], 1,
    'Unlimited liability means there is no legal separation between the owner and the business. If the business cannot pay its debts, the owner\'s personal assets (house, car, savings) can be used to pay them.'),
  t(5, '### Partnership\n\nA partnership is a business owned by **2 to 20 people** who agree to run a business together and share the profits and losses.\n\n**Characteristics:**\n- Governed by a **Partnership Agreement** (should be written, but can be oral)\n- Partners share profits, losses, and management duties as agreed\n- No separate legal personality (like a sole trader, but with multiple owners)\n- **Unlimited liability** for ALL partners — each partner can be held liable for the FULL debts of the partnership\n- If one partner dies or withdraws, the partnership dissolves\n\n**The Partnership Agreement should include:**\n- Names and capital contributions of each partner\n- How profits and losses will be shared\n- Duties and responsibilities of each partner\n- How decisions will be made\n- Procedures for adding or removing partners\n- What happens if a partner dies or retires'),
  t(6, '### Advantages and Disadvantages of a Partnership\n\n| Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|------------|---------------|\n| More capital available (combined resources) | **Unlimited liability** for ALL partners |\n| Combined skills and expertise | Partners may disagree on decisions |\n| Shared workload | Profits must be shared |\n| Easy to form (no formal registration) | Actions of ONE partner bind ALL partners |\n| Partners are taxed individually (no double tax) | Partnership dissolves when a partner dies/leaves |\n| Better decision-making from combined experience | Slow decision-making if partners disagree |\n| Privacy (no published accounts) | Trust is essential — one bad partner can ruin it |\n\n**Examples of partnerships in SA:** Law firms, medical practices, accounting firms, veterinary practices.\n\n**Example:** Two architects in Cape Town form a partnership. One specialises in residential design, the other in commercial projects. They share costs, clients, and profits, but if one architect makes a costly mistake, BOTH partners are personally liable.'),
  fb(7, 'A sole proprietor has ___ liability and no separate ___ personality from the business.',
    ['unlimited', 'legal'],
    'A sole proprietor has unlimited liability (personal assets are at risk) and no separate legal personality (the owner and business are the same entity in law).',
    ['Think about whether the owner\'s personal belongings are protected.']),
  q(8, 'What is the maximum number of partners allowed in a partnership in South Africa?',
    ['5', '10', '20', '50'], 2,
    'South African law allows a maximum of 20 partners in a partnership. If more than 20 people want to go into business together, they must form a company.'),
  t(9, '### Comparing Sole Trader and Partnership\n\n| Feature | Sole Proprietor | Partnership |\n|---------|----------------|-------------|\n| Owners | 1 | 2-20 |\n| Legal personality | No | No |\n| Liability | Unlimited (owner) | Unlimited (ALL partners) |\n| Continuity | None | None (dissolves on death) |\n| Capital | Limited (own savings) | Combined resources |\n| Decision-making | Fast (owner decides) | Shared (may be slower) |\n| Skills | Limited to owner | Combined expertise |\n| Profit | Kept by owner | Shared as per agreement |\n| Risk | Owner bears alone | Shared but each liable for all |\n| Formation | Informal | Partnership agreement |\n\n**Important:** In both forms, the owners have **no legal separation** from the business. Their personal assets are at risk if the business fails.'),
  q(10, 'A key advantage of a partnership over a sole trader is:',
    ['Limited liability', 'Continuity after death', 'More capital and combined skills', 'No need to share profits'], 2,
    'A partnership pools the capital and skills of multiple people, giving it more resources than a sole trader. However, it still has unlimited liability and no continuity, like a sole trader.'),
];

// Lesson 2: Close Corporation, Companies, Co-operatives
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '### Close Corporation (CC)\n\nA close corporation is a business entity with **1 to 10 members**.\n\n**Important:** Since 2011 (when the new Companies Act came into effect), **no new close corporations can be registered** in South Africa. However, existing CCs are allowed to continue operating.\n\n**Characteristics:**\n- **Separate legal personality** — the CC is a separate legal entity from its members\n- **Limited liability** — members\' personal assets are generally protected from business debts\n- Governed by a **Founding Statement** registered with CIPC\n- Members manage the CC directly (no board of directors)\n- Simpler compliance requirements than a company\n\n| Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|------------|---------------|\n| Separate legal personality | Cannot register new CCs since 2011 |\n| Limited liability for members | Maximum 10 members |\n| Simpler than a company | Harder to transfer member interests |\n| Continuity (does not die with members) | Less credibility than Pty Ltd |\n| Members manage directly | Still must file annual returns with CIPC |'),
  t(2, '### Private Company — (Pty) Ltd\n\nA private company is the most common form of formal business registration in South Africa.\n\n**Characteristics:**\n- Registered under the **Companies Act 71 of 2008** with CIPC\n- Designated as **(Pty) Ltd** (Proprietary Limited)\n- **Separate legal personality** — the company is a separate entity from its shareholders\n- **Limited liability** — shareholders\' personal assets are protected\n- Minimum 1 director; no maximum number of shareholders\n- Cannot sell shares to the public\n- Must have a **Memorandum of Incorporation (MOI)** — the founding document\n\n**The MOI includes:**\n- Company name and type\n- Rights and duties of shareholders and directors\n- Rules for issuing and transferring shares\n- Procedures for meetings and decision-making\n\n**Example:** Many medium and large South African businesses operate as (Pty) Ltd companies: Checkers (Pty) Ltd, Woolworths (Pty) Ltd before it listed, and countless small businesses.'),
  t(3, '### Public Company — Ltd\n\nA public company can sell its shares to the **general public** through the Johannesburg Stock Exchange (JSE).\n\n**Characteristics:**\n- Designated as **Ltd** (Limited)\n- **Separate legal personality** and **limited liability**\n- Minimum 3 directors\n- Shares freely tradeable on the JSE\n- Must publish annual audited financial statements\n- Subject to strict regulation by the JSE and Companies Act\n- Must comply with **King IV** principles on good corporate governance\n\n| Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|------------|---------------|\n| Access to large capital (public share sales) | Very expensive to set up and maintain |\n| Greater credibility and prestige | Shareholders may lose control (ownership spread) |\n| Attracts top talent | Must publish financial information publicly |\n| Limited liability for shareholders | Complex regulatory requirements |\n| Continuity (lives beyond individual shareholders) | Vulnerable to hostile takeovers |\n\n**JSE-listed examples:** Shoprite Holdings Ltd, MTN Group Ltd, Standard Bank Group Ltd, Discovery Ltd, Naspers Ltd, Sasol Ltd'),
  q(4, 'What is the main difference between a private company (Pty) Ltd and a public company (Ltd)?',
    ['Private companies have unlimited liability', 'Public companies can sell shares to the general public on the JSE', 'Private companies cannot have more than 10 shareholders', 'Public companies do not need directors'], 1,
    'The key difference is that a public company can sell shares to the general public (typically through the JSE), while a private company cannot offer shares publicly.'),
  t(5, '### Non-Profit Company (NPC)\n\nA non-profit company is formed to pursue a public benefit objective, not to generate profit for its members.\n\n**Characteristics:**\n- Registered as NPC under the Companies Act\n- Any income must be used to advance its stated objective\n- Members and directors do not receive profits or dividends\n- May apply for tax exemption from SARS\n- Must have at least 3 directors\n\n**Examples:** Charity organisations, religious bodies, community development organisations, sports associations.\n\n### Co-operative\n\nA co-operative is owned and managed by a group of people with a common interest who share the benefits equally.\n\n**Characteristics:**\n- Democratic management: **one member, one vote** (regardless of contribution)\n- Minimum 5 members\n- Members share profits equally (or based on participation)\n- Must register with CIPC under the Co-operatives Act\n\n**Types:**\n- **Consumer co-operative** — members buy goods at lower prices\n- **Producer co-operative** — farmers combine to market produce\n- **Worker co-operative** — owned and managed by employees\n- **Financial co-operative** — savings and credit (stokvels)'),
  q(6, 'How are decisions made in a co-operative?',
    ['By the largest shareholder', 'Democratically — one member, one vote', 'By the board of directors only', 'By government appointment'], 1,
    'Co-operatives follow the democratic principle of one member, one vote, regardless of how much each member has contributed.'),
  t(7, '### Comparison of All Forms of Ownership\n\n| Feature | Sole Trader | Partnership | CC | Pty Ltd | Ltd | Co-operative |\n|---------|------------|-------------|-----|---------|-----|-------------|\n| Legal personality | No | No | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Liability | Unlimited | Unlimited | Limited | Limited | Limited | Limited |\n| Max owners | 1 | 20 | 10 | No max | No max | No max |\n| Decision speed | Fast | Medium | Medium | Medium | Slow | Slow |\n| Capital access | Low | Low-Med | Medium | Medium | High | Medium |\n| Continuity | None | None | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Start-up cost | Very low | Low | N/A* | Medium | High | Medium |\n| Privacy | High | High | Medium | Medium | Low | Medium |\n\n*No new CCs can be registered since 2011.\n\n**Key takeaway:** The choice of business form depends on the size of the business, the number of owners, the level of risk, the capital needed, and the desired level of control.'),
  fb(8, 'A company with separate legal personality and limited liability that CANNOT sell shares to the public is called a ___ company, abbreviated as ___.',
    ['private', '(Pty) Ltd'],
    'A private company (Pty) Ltd has separate legal personality and limited liability but cannot offer its shares to the general public.',
    ['Think about the type of company that is not listed on the stock exchange.']),
  t(9, '### The Companies Act 71 of 2008\n\nThe Companies Act is the primary legislation governing companies in South Africa.\n\n**Key provisions for Grade 10:**\n\n| Provision | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Registration** | All companies must register with CIPC and file an MOI |\n| **Directors** | Must act in good faith and in the best interest of the company |\n| **Annual returns** | Companies must file annual returns with CIPC |\n| **Financial statements** | Public companies must have audited financial statements |\n| **Business rescue** | Companies in financial difficulty can apply for protection from creditors while being restructured |\n| **Deregistration** | Inactive companies can be deregistered by CIPC |\n\n**CIPC (Companies and Intellectual Property Commission):**\n- Registers companies, CCs, co-operatives, and intellectual property\n- Maintains a public register of all registered businesses\n- Processes annual returns and amendments\n- Enforces the Companies Act'),
  q(10, 'Which organisation is responsible for registering companies in South Africa?',
    ['SARS', 'SABS', 'CIPC', 'CCMA'], 2,
    'CIPC (Companies and Intellectual Property Commission) is responsible for registering companies, close corporations, co-operatives, and intellectual property in South Africa.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 9: Creative Thinking and Problem Solving (Term 3)
// =============================================================================

blockNum = 0;
const ch9_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Creative Thinking in Business\n\nCreative thinking is the ability to come up with new, original, and useful ideas. In business, creative thinking helps entrepreneurs identify opportunities, solve problems, and stay ahead of competitors.\n\n### Creative Thinking vs Problem Solving\n\n| Creative Thinking | Problem Solving |\n|-------------------|----------------|\n| Generating new ideas | Finding solutions to existing problems |\n| Looking for opportunities | Addressing challenges |\n| "What could we create?" | "How do we fix this?" |\n| Divergent thinking (many answers) | Convergent thinking (best answer) |\n| Proactive (looking forward) | Reactive (addressing current issues) |\n\n**Both are essential:** Creative thinking generates ideas, and problem solving turns them into practical solutions.\n\n**Why creative thinking matters in business:**\n- Generates innovative products and services\n- Identifies new business opportunities\n- Solves problems in creative ways\n- Differentiates the business from competitors\n- Adapts the business to changing environments'),
  t(2, '### The Advantages of Creative Thinking\n\nCreative thinking gives businesses a competitive edge:\n\n**For entrepreneurs:**\n- Helps identify gaps in the market\n- Generates unique business ideas\n- Enables innovative solutions to start-up challenges\n- Creates differentiation from competitors\n\n**For existing businesses:**\n- Leads to new products and services\n- Improves existing processes and systems\n- Helps adapt to changing market conditions\n- Attracts and retains innovative employees\n- Drives growth and expansion\n\n**Example:** The founders of Nando\'s used creative thinking to differentiate their restaurant from competitors. Instead of competing directly with KFC on fried chicken, they created a unique positioning around Portuguese-style flame-grilled peri-peri chicken with a distinctly South African, humorous brand personality. This creative approach made them a global success.'),
  t(3, '### Problem-Solving Techniques\n\n**The problem-solving process:**\n\n1. **Identify the problem** — What exactly is wrong? Gather facts.\n2. **Analyse the problem** — What are the causes? Why is it happening?\n3. **Generate possible solutions** — Brainstorm multiple options without judging.\n4. **Evaluate solutions** — Assess each option against criteria (cost, time, feasibility).\n5. **Choose the best solution** — Select the most practical and effective option.\n6. **Implement the solution** — Create a plan and carry it out.\n7. **Review the results** — Did it work? Should anything be adjusted?\n\n**Key techniques for generating solutions:**\n\n| Technique | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Brainstorming** | Group generates many ideas quickly without criticism |\n| **Mind mapping** | Visual diagram connecting related ideas around a central topic |\n| **Force-field analysis** | Weighs forces for and against a proposed change |\n| **Delphi technique** | Anonymous expert opinions gathered in multiple rounds |\n| **Nominal group technique** | Individuals write ideas independently, then vote |\n| **SCAMPER** | Systematic checklist: Substitute, Combine, Adapt, Modify, Put to other use, Eliminate, Reverse |'),
  q(4, 'What is the FIRST step in the problem-solving process?',
    ['Generate possible solutions', 'Identify and define the problem', 'Implement the solution', 'Evaluate alternatives'], 1,
    'The first step is to clearly identify and define the problem. You cannot solve a problem effectively if you do not understand exactly what it is.'),
  t(5, '### Brainstorming\n\nBrainstorming is a group creativity technique for generating a large number of ideas in a short time.\n\n**Rules of brainstorming:**\n1. **No criticism** — Do not judge or evaluate ideas during the brainstorming session\n2. **Quantity over quality** — Generate as many ideas as possible\n3. **Build on others\' ideas** — "Yes, and..." rather than "No, but..."\n4. **Wild ideas welcome** — Unusual ideas can spark practical solutions\n5. **Record everything** — Write down every idea\n\n**Steps:**\n1. Define the problem or question clearly\n2. Set a time limit (e.g., 15-30 minutes)\n3. Each person shares ideas freely\n4. Record all ideas on a whiteboard or paper\n5. After brainstorming, evaluate and group ideas\n6. Select the most promising ideas for further development\n\n**Example:** A small restaurant in Johannesburg is losing customers. In a brainstorming session, the team generates ideas: delivery service, themed menu nights, cooking classes, loyalty cards, social media competitions, live music, partnerships with food bloggers. After evaluation, they choose delivery service and social media as the best first steps.'),
  t(6, '### Force-Field Analysis and the Delphi Technique\n\n**Force-field analysis** (developed by Kurt Lewin) identifies forces FOR and AGAINST a proposed change:\n\n1. State the proposed change\n2. List **driving forces** (pushing for change) and rate their strength (1-5)\n3. List **restraining forces** (pushing against change) and rate their strength (1-5)\n4. Compare totals: if driving forces are stronger, the change is likely to succeed\n\n**Example: Should a tuck shop owner add a delivery service?**\n\n| Driving Forces (FOR) | Score | Restraining Forces (AGAINST) | Score |\n|---------------------|-------|------------------------------|-------|\n| Customers want delivery | 5 | Cost of hiring a driver | 4 |\n| Competitors offer delivery | 4 | Need to buy a vehicle | 3 |\n| Increase in sales | 4 | Risk of deliveries being late | 2 |\n| **Total** | **13** | **Total** | **9** |\n\nDriving forces outweigh restraining forces — the delivery service should proceed.\n\n**The Delphi technique** uses anonymous expert opinions gathered over multiple rounds until consensus is reached. Experts never meet face-to-face, which avoids groupthink.'),
  fb(7, 'In force-field analysis, ___ forces push for change and ___ forces push against change.',
    ['driving', 'restraining'],
    'Driving forces are the reasons supporting the change. Restraining forces are the barriers or arguments against the change.',
    ['Think about what pushes the change forward and what holds it back.']),
  q(8, 'Which brainstorming rule states that unusual and wild ideas should be encouraged?',
    ['No criticism during the session', 'Quantity over quality', 'Build on others\' ideas', 'Wild ideas are welcome'], 3,
    'The "wild ideas welcome" rule encourages participants to think without limits. Sometimes the most unusual ideas spark creative practical solutions.'),
  t(9, '### Applying Creative Thinking to Business Opportunities\n\nEntrepreneurs use creative thinking to identify and develop business opportunities:\n\n**How to spot opportunities:**\n- Listen to customer complaints (every complaint is a potential opportunity)\n- Observe trends in technology, lifestyle, and demographics\n- Look at what is successful overseas and adapt it for South Africa\n- Identify inefficiencies in existing businesses\n- Combine two existing ideas into something new\n\n**South African examples of creative business ideas:**\n- **Checkers Sixty60** — Combined grocery shopping with rapid delivery (observed the global trend of quick commerce)\n- **Yoco** — Identified that small SA businesses needed affordable card payment solutions\n- **SweepSouth** — Created an app connecting domestic workers with households (like Uber for cleaning)\n- **Aerobotics** — Uses drones and AI to help farmers monitor crop health\n\n**The SCAMPER checklist:**\n- **S**ubstitute — What can be replaced?\n- **C**ombine — What can be merged?\n- **A**dapt — What can be borrowed from elsewhere?\n- **M**odify — What can be enlarged, reduced, or changed?\n- **P**ut to other use — Can it serve a different purpose?\n- **E**liminate — What can be removed?\n- **R**everse/Rearrange — What if you did it backward or in a different order?'),
  q(10, 'SweepSouth\'s business model of connecting domestic workers with households via an app is an example of:',
    ['Routine thinking', 'Problem avoidance', 'Creative thinking applied to a business opportunity', 'Government regulation'], 2,
    'SweepSouth identified a gap in the market and used creative thinking to develop an innovative platform connecting domestic workers with households, using technology to solve a real problem.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 10: Business Opportunity and Related Factors (Term 3)
// =============================================================================

blockNum = 0;
const ch10_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Business Opportunities\n\n### What Is a Business Opportunity?\n\nA business opportunity is a gap in the market where a consumer need is not being met, or not being met well enough. Identifying and developing opportunities is the foundation of entrepreneurship.\n\n**Sources of business opportunities:**\n\n| Source | Description | Example |\n|--------|-------------|----------|\n| **Consumer needs** | Something people want or need but cannot easily get | Affordable healthy meals in a food desert |\n| **Changing trends** | Shifts in lifestyle, technology, or demographics | Rise of plant-based foods |\n| **Problems** | Existing frustrations or pain points | Traffic congestion → ride-sharing apps |\n| **Government policy** | New laws creating demand for products/services | POPIA → demand for data protection consultants |\n| **Technology** | New tech enabling new business models | Smartphones → mobile banking |\n| **Import substitution** | Making locally what is currently imported | Local manufacturing of hand sanitiser during COVID-19 |'),
  t(2, '### Market Research\n\nBefore pursuing a business opportunity, entrepreneurs must conduct market research to determine if there is real demand.\n\n**Types of market research:**\n\n| Type | Description | Methods |\n|------|-------------|----------|\n| **Primary research** | New data collected directly from the source | Surveys, interviews, focus groups, observation |\n| **Secondary research** | Existing data collected by others | Statistics SA, industry reports, internet research, newspapers |\n\n**Key questions market research answers:**\n1. Who are the potential customers?\n2. How large is the market?\n3. What do customers want and need?\n4. How much are customers willing to pay?\n5. Who are the competitors and what do they offer?\n6. What are the trends in the industry?\n\n**Example:** Before launching a tutoring business, an entrepreneur surveys parents in the area to find out which subjects their children need help with, how much they would pay per hour, and whether they prefer in-person or online sessions.'),
  t(3, '### SWOT Analysis for Evaluating Opportunities\n\nSWOT analysis helps entrepreneurs evaluate whether a business opportunity is viable.\n\n| | Positive | Negative |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Internal** | **Strengths** (What advantages do I have?) | **Weaknesses** (What do I lack?) |\n| **External** | **Opportunities** (What market gaps exist?) | **Threats** (What could go wrong?) |\n\n**Example: Opening a mobile car wash in Centurion**\n\n| Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|-----------|------------|\n| Low start-up costs | No established brand |\n| Flexible schedule | Dependent on good weather |\n| Personal, convenient service | Limited to local area |\n\n| Opportunities | Threats |\n|---------------|----------|\n| Busy professionals need convenience | Other car wash businesses in the area |\n| Water-saving methods appeal to eco-conscious customers | Water restrictions during drought |\n| Can expand to fleet management contracts | Economic downturn reducing demand for services |'),
  q(4, 'What is the main purpose of conducting market research before starting a business?',
    ['To register the business with CIPC', 'To determine if there is real demand for the product or service', 'To calculate the owner\'s salary', 'To choose the business name'], 1,
    'Market research helps the entrepreneur determine whether there is genuine demand for the product or service, understand customers, and assess the competition before investing time and money.'),
  t(5, '### Factors to Consider When Starting a Business\n\nSeveral factors influence whether a business opportunity is worth pursuing:\n\n**1. Financial factors:**\n- How much capital is needed to start?\n- Where will the funding come from? (savings, loans, investors)\n- How long before the business becomes profitable?\n- What are the ongoing costs (rent, salaries, stock, utilities)?\n\n**2. Market factors:**\n- Is the target market large enough?\n- Are there existing competitors? How strong are they?\n- Is the demand growing, stable, or declining?\n\n**3. Personal factors:**\n- Do you have the skills and knowledge needed?\n- Are you willing to take the risk?\n- Can you handle the workload and uncertainty?\n\n**4. Location factors:**\n- Is the location accessible to customers?\n- What are the rental costs?\n- Is there enough parking and foot traffic?\n\n**5. Legal factors:**\n- What licences and permits are needed?\n- Must the business comply with specific regulations (e.g., health permits for food)?\n- How will it be registered (sole trader, Pty Ltd, etc.)?'),
  fb(6, 'Before starting a business, an entrepreneur must conduct ___ research to determine if there is genuine demand and understand the target ___.',
    ['market', 'market'],
    'Market research helps entrepreneurs understand the target market, customer needs, competition, and demand before investing in a business.',
    ['Think about researching the people who might buy your product.']),
  t(7, '### Sources of Funding for New Businesses\n\nEntrepreneurs need capital to start their businesses. Here are the main funding sources available in South Africa:\n\n| Source | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Personal savings** | Owner\'s own money — no interest, but limited amount |\n| **Family and friends** | Informal loans or investments — flexible but risky for relationships |\n| **Bank loans** | Formal loans from banks — larger amounts but require collateral and interest |\n| **Government agencies** | SEDA (advice), SEFA (finance), NEF (black empowerment), NYDA (youth) |\n| **Venture capital** | Professional investors who provide large capital in exchange for equity |\n| **Angel investors** | Wealthy individuals who invest in startups, often with mentorship |\n| **Crowdfunding** | Raising small amounts from many people online (Thundafund, BackaBuddy) |\n| **Stokvels** | Community savings groups that pool and distribute funds |\n\n**Government support agencies:**\n- **SEDA** — Free business advice, mentoring, and training\n- **SEFA** — Loans for small businesses\n- **NYDA** — Supports young entrepreneurs (18-35) with grants and training\n- **IDC** — Funding for industrial and manufacturing projects'),
  q(8, 'Which South African government agency provides free business advice and mentoring to small businesses?',
    ['SEFA', 'SARS', 'SEDA', 'CIPC'], 2,
    'SEDA (Small Enterprise Development Agency) provides free business advice, mentoring, business plan development, and training programmes to small businesses.'),
  t(9, '### Steps to Starting a Business Venture\n\n**Step 1: Identify an opportunity** — Spot a gap in the market\n\n**Step 2: Conduct research** — Market research and feasibility study\n\n**Step 3: Write a business plan** — A detailed document covering your strategy\n\n**Step 4: Choose a form of ownership** — Sole trader, partnership, or company\n\n**Step 5: Secure funding** — Personal savings, loans, grants, or investors\n\n**Step 6: Register the business:**\n- Register with **CIPC** (if forming a company or CC)\n- Register with **SARS** for tax (income tax, VAT if turnover exceeds R1 million)\n- Register for **UIF** and **COIDA** if hiring employees\n- Obtain necessary licences (e.g., liquor licence, health certificate)\n\n**Step 7: Set up operations** — Secure premises, buy equipment, hire staff\n\n**Step 8: Launch** — Open for business and implement your marketing plan\n\n**Key legal registrations:**\n- CIPC for company registration\n- SARS for tax (IT, VAT, PAYE, UIF)\n- Department of Labour for COIDA\n- Local municipality for business licence'),
  q(10, 'An entrepreneur who wants to start a food business must register with SARS for tax and also obtain a:',
    ['Mining licence', 'Health certificate from the municipality', 'Stock exchange listing', 'BBBEE certificate'], 1,
    'Food businesses must obtain a health certificate (Certificate of Acceptability) from the local municipality to ensure they meet hygiene and food safety standards.'),
];

