blockNum = 0;
const ch11_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Presentation of Business Information\n\n### Why Presentation Skills Matter\n\nIn business, being able to present information clearly and convincingly is essential. Business people present information to:\n- Pitch ideas to investors and banks\n- Report to management and stakeholders\n- Train and instruct employees\n- Sell products and services to customers\n- Communicate research findings\n\n### Types of Communication\n\n| Type | Description | Examples |\n|------|-------------|----------|\n| **Verbal** | Spoken words | Presentations, meetings, phone calls |\n| **Non-verbal** | Body language and visual cues | Facial expressions, posture, gestures, eye contact |\n| **Written** | Words on paper or screen | Reports, emails, memos, business plans |\n| **Visual** | Images and graphics | Charts, graphs, infographics, photographs |\n\nAn effective presenter combines all four types to communicate their message clearly.'),
  t(2, '### Verbal Presentation Skills\n\nVerbal communication is what you SAY and HOW you say it.\n\n**Key verbal skills:**\n\n| Skill | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Clarity** | Speak clearly; use simple, understandable words |\n| **Pace** | Not too fast (audience cannot follow) or too slow (audience gets bored) |\n| **Volume** | Loud enough for everyone to hear; vary volume for emphasis |\n| **Tone** | Change your tone to keep the audience interested and convey emotion |\n| **Pausing** | Pause after key points to let the audience absorb information |\n| **Language** | Use words appropriate for your audience — avoid jargon with non-experts |\n\n**Common mistakes:**\n- Using filler words ("um", "uh", "like", "you know")\n- Reading directly from notes or slides\n- Speaking in a monotone voice\n- Talking too fast when nervous\n- Using language the audience does not understand'),
  t(3, '### Non-Verbal Presentation Skills\n\nResearch shows that **up to 55%** of communication is non-verbal. Your body language often speaks louder than your words.\n\n**Key non-verbal skills:**\n\n| Skill | Do | Do Not |\n|-------|-----|--------|\n| **Eye contact** | Look at different sections of the audience | Stare at the floor, ceiling, or one person |\n| **Posture** | Stand straight and confident | Slouch, lean, or sway |\n| **Gestures** | Use natural hand movements to emphasise points | Fidget, play with hair, or keep hands in pockets |\n| **Movement** | Move purposefully across the space | Pace nervously or stand rigidly |\n| **Facial expressions** | Smile and show enthusiasm | Look bored, anxious, or annoyed |\n| **Appearance** | Dress appropriately for the audience | Wear distracting or inappropriate clothing |'),
  q(4, 'What percentage of communication is estimated to be non-verbal?',
    ['10%', '25%', '55%', '90%'], 2,
    'Research suggests that up to 55% of communication is non-verbal (body language), about 38% is vocal tone, and only 7% is the actual words spoken.'),
  t(5, '### Visual Aids\n\nVisual aids support your presentation and help the audience understand and remember your message.\n\n**Types of visual aids:**\n\n| Visual Aid | Best Used For |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| **PowerPoint/Google Slides** | Most business presentations |\n| **Charts and graphs** | Displaying data and trends |\n| **Handouts** | Detailed information for reference |\n| **Posters and banners** | Advertising and exhibitions |\n| **Videos** | Demonstrating processes or products |\n| **Flip charts/Whiteboards** | Interactive discussions |\n| **Physical samples** | Product demonstrations |\n\n**Rules for effective slides:**\n- Maximum 6 lines of text per slide\n- Use large, readable fonts (minimum size 24)\n- One main idea per slide\n- Use images and diagrams more than text\n- Keep a consistent design and colour scheme\n- Do NOT read slides word-for-word to the audience\n\n**Charts and graphs:**\n- **Bar charts** — compare quantities\n- **Pie charts** — show proportions of a whole\n- **Line graphs** — show trends over time\n- **Tables** — present detailed data clearly'),
  fb(6, 'The two main categories of presentation skills are ___ communication (what you say) and ___ communication (body language).',
    ['verbal', 'non-verbal'],
    'Verbal communication involves spoken words, while non-verbal communication involves body language, gestures, facial expressions, and physical appearance.',
    ['Think about the words you speak versus how your body communicates.']),
  t(7, '### Planning a Presentation\n\n**Steps to prepare an effective presentation:**\n\n1. **Know your audience** — Who are they? What do they need? What language do they speak?\n2. **Define your purpose** — Are you informing, persuading, instructing, or motivating?\n3. **Research your topic** — Gather accurate, relevant information\n4. **Organise your content** — Use a clear structure:\n   - **Introduction (10%):** Greet, introduce yourself, state your purpose\n   - **Body (80%):** Present main points with evidence and examples\n   - **Conclusion (10%):** Summarise key points, recommend next steps\n5. **Prepare visual aids** — Create slides, handouts, or other materials\n6. **Practise** — Rehearse multiple times; time yourself\n7. **Check the venue** — Test equipment, check seating, ensure audibility\n\n**The "Tell them" rule:**\n- Tell them what you are going to tell them (introduction)\n- Tell them (body)\n- Tell them what you told them (conclusion)'),
  q(8, 'What is the recommended structure for a business presentation?',
    ['Conclusion first, then body, then introduction', 'All content should be in the body only', 'Introduction (10%), Body (80%), Conclusion (10%)', 'Equal time for introduction, body, and conclusion'], 2,
    'The recommended structure is Introduction (10%), Body (80%), and Conclusion (10%). The introduction hooks the audience, the body delivers the content, and the conclusion summarises and recommends.'),
  t(9, '### Different Presentation Formats\n\nBusiness information can be presented in various formats:\n\n**Verbal presentations:**\n- Boardroom presentations to management\n- Sales pitches to clients\n- Training sessions for employees\n\n**Written presentations:**\n- **Business reports** — formal documents presenting research findings or proposals\n- **Executive summaries** — condensed overview of a longer report\n- **Memos** — internal communication within the business\n- **Business letters** — formal external communication\n\n**Visual presentations:**\n- **Infographics** — combine text and graphics to explain complex information\n- **Posters and banners** — used at exhibitions, trade shows, and conferences\n- **Dashboards** — visual displays of key business metrics\n\n**Key principle:** Always choose the presentation format that best suits your audience and purpose. A sales pitch to a customer requires different skills and materials than a financial report to the board of directors.'),
  q(10, 'Which visual aid would be MOST appropriate for showing how a company\'s sales have changed over the past five years?',
    ['A pie chart', 'A line graph', 'A table', 'A poster'], 1,
    'A line graph is the most appropriate visual aid for showing trends over time. It clearly shows how sales have increased, decreased, or fluctuated over the five-year period.'),
];

