blockNum = 0;
const ch12_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Business Plan\n\n### What Is a Business Plan?\n\nA business plan is a written document that describes a business, its goals, its strategies for achieving those goals, and the financial projections for the future.\n\n**Why a business plan is important:**\n- **For the entrepreneur:** Provides a roadmap for starting and growing the business\n- **For investors/banks:** Shows that the business idea is viable and worth funding\n- **For management:** Guides decision-making and measures progress\n- **For partners/stakeholders:** Communicates the vision and strategy\n\n**When is a business plan needed?**\n- Starting a new business\n- Applying for a bank loan or investment\n- Expanding an existing business\n- Attracting new partners or shareholders\n- Planning strategic changes\n\nA good business plan is realistic, detailed, and based on proper research — not guesswork or wishful thinking.'),
  t(2, '### Components of a Business Plan\n\nA complete business plan includes the following sections:\n\n| Section | Purpose |\n|---------|----------|\n| **1. Cover page** | Business name, logo, contact details, date |\n| **2. Executive summary** | Brief overview of the entire plan (write this LAST) |\n| **3. Business description** | What the business does, its mission, vision, and values |\n| **4. Market analysis** | Target market, customer profile, competitors, industry trends |\n| **5. Marketing plan** | Product, pricing, promotion, and distribution strategies |\n| **6. Operations plan** | Location, production process, suppliers, technology |\n| **7. Management team** | Key people, their qualifications, and roles |\n| **8. Financial plan** | Start-up costs, income projections, cash flow, break-even |\n| **9. Funding request** | How much money is needed, how it will be used, and repayment plan |\n| **10. Appendices** | Supporting documents (CVs, permits, contracts, research data) |\n\nEach section builds the case that the business idea is viable, the team is capable, and the investment is worthwhile.'),
  t(3, '### The Executive Summary\n\nThe executive summary is the MOST important section of the business plan. It is a condensed version of the entire plan, usually 1-2 pages.\n\n**What to include:**\n- The business concept (what the business does)\n- The product or service offered\n- The target market and customer need\n- The competitive advantage (why customers will choose you)\n- Key financial highlights (start-up costs, projected revenue)\n- The funding required and how it will be used\n- The management team\'s qualifications\n\n**Important tips:**\n- Write the executive summary LAST (after all other sections are complete)\n- Keep it clear and concise — an investor should understand the opportunity in 5 minutes\n- Make it compelling — this section determines whether the reader continues\n\n**Example:** "GreenBox is a Johannesburg-based meal delivery service providing affordable, healthy, plant-based meals to office workers in Sandton. The growing trend towards health-conscious eating and convenience creates a R2 billion opportunity in the Gauteng market. We require R250 000 in start-up capital to launch operations within 3 months."'),
  q(4, 'Why should the executive summary be written LAST?',
    ['It is the least important section', 'It summarises all other sections so they must be written first', 'It should be the longest section', 'Banks do not read executive summaries'], 1,
    'The executive summary is a condensed overview of the entire business plan. You need to complete all other sections first before you can accurately summarise them.'),
  t(5, '### Market Analysis and Marketing Plan\n\n**Market analysis** examines the market the business will operate in:\n\n- **Target market:** Who are your customers? (age, income, location, interests)\n- **Market size:** How many potential customers exist?\n- **Customer needs:** What problems does your product solve?\n- **Competitor analysis:** Who else serves this market? What are their strengths and weaknesses?\n- **Industry trends:** Is the market growing or declining?\n\n**Marketing plan** describes how you will attract and keep customers (the 4 Ps):\n\n| P | Question | Example |\n|---|---------|----------|\n| **Product** | What will you sell? | Healthy meal boxes with 3 options |\n| **Price** | How much will customers pay? | R65 per meal box |\n| **Place** | Where/how will customers buy it? | App-based delivery in Sandton |\n| **Promotion** | How will customers hear about it? | Instagram, office flyers, free tastings |'),
  t(6, '### Financial Plan\n\nThe financial plan proves that the business can make money. It is the section that investors and banks scrutinise most carefully.\n\n**Key components:**\n\n**1. Start-up costs:** Everything needed to launch\n\n| Item | Estimated Cost |\n|------|---------------|\n| Equipment (kitchen, packaging) | R80 000 |\n| Initial stock (ingredients) | R15 000 |\n| Branding and marketing | R20 000 |\n| Delivery vehicle deposit | R30 000 |\n| Registration and licences | R5 000 |\n| Working capital (3 months) | R100 000 |\n| **Total** | **R250 000** |\n\n**2. Revenue projections:** Expected income per month/year\n\n**3. Cash flow projection:** Money flowing in and out each month\n\n**4. Break-even analysis:** When will income equal expenses?\n- Break-even point = Fixed costs ÷ (Selling price per unit − Variable cost per unit)\n- Example: Fixed costs R20 000/month, selling price R65, variable cost R30\n- Break-even = R20 000 ÷ (R65 − R30) = 572 meals per month'),
  fb(7, 'The break-even point is calculated by dividing ___ costs by the difference between the selling price and the ___ cost per unit.',
    ['fixed', 'variable'],
    'Break-even = Fixed costs ÷ (Selling price − Variable cost). At this point, total revenue equals total costs and the business makes neither profit nor loss.',
    ['Think about which costs stay the same regardless of sales, and which costs change with each unit.']),
  q(8, 'If a business has fixed costs of R10 000, a selling price of R50, and variable costs of R25 per unit, how many units must it sell to break even?',
    ['200', '400', '500', '250'], 1,
    'Break-even = Fixed costs ÷ (Selling price − Variable cost) = R10 000 ÷ (R50 − R25) = R10 000 ÷ R25 = 400 units.'),
  t(9, '### Operations and Management Sections\n\n**Operations plan** describes how the business will function day-to-day:\n- Location and premises (where the business will operate)\n- Equipment and technology needed\n- Production process or service delivery method\n- Suppliers and supply chain\n- Quality control measures\n- Operating hours\n\n**Management team** introduces the key people:\n- Owner/founder qualifications and experience\n- Key management positions and who fills them\n- Skills gaps and how they will be addressed\n- External advisors (accountant, lawyer, mentor)\n\n**A strong management team section answers:**\n- Why is this team capable of making this business succeed?\n- What relevant experience does each person bring?\n- How will they handle challenges?\n\n**Example:** "The GreenBox management team includes: Nomsa Dlamini (founder and CEO, 5 years in food service management at Woolworths), Sipho Mabaso (Head Chef, qualified from Capsicum Culinary Studio with 3 years experience), and an external accountant for financial management."'),
  q(10, 'Which section of the business plan do investors and banks typically scrutinise MOST carefully?',
    ['Business description', 'Marketing plan', 'Financial plan', 'Cover page'], 2,
    'The financial plan is scrutinised most carefully because it proves whether the business can generate enough revenue to cover costs, repay loans, and make a profit. Investors need to see realistic financial projections.'),
];

