blockNum = 0;
const ch14_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Grade 10 Business Studies Revision\n\n### Exam Structure\n\nThe Grade 10 Business Studies examination typically consists of two papers:\n\n**Paper 1 — Business Environments (2 hours, 150 marks):**\n- Section A: Short questions (compulsory) — 30 marks\n- Section B: THREE direct/indirect questions (answer TWO) — 60 marks\n- Section C: ONE essay (choose from TWO options) — 40 marks\n\n**Paper 2 — Business Ventures and Roles (2 hours, 150 marks):**\n- Section A: Short questions (compulsory) — 30 marks\n- Section B: THREE direct/indirect questions (answer TWO) — 60 marks\n- Section C: ONE essay (choose from TWO options) — 40 marks\n\n**Paper 1 topics:** Micro environment, business functions, market environment, macro environment (PESTLE), interrelationship between environments, business sectors, socio-economic issues, social responsibility, forms of ownership\n\n**Paper 2 topics:** Creative thinking and problem solving, business opportunities, presentation of business information, business plan, relationships and team performance'),
  t(2, '### Key Action Verbs\n\nUnderstanding what the question asks is essential for scoring marks:\n\n| Verb | What to Do | Marks Guide |\n|------|-----------|-------------|\n| **Name/State** | Give the name only — no explanation | 1 mark each |\n| **List** | Write items one below the other | 1 mark each |\n| **Define** | Give the meaning of the concept | 2 marks |\n| **Describe** | Give a detailed account | 2 marks per point |\n| **Explain** | Make clear by giving reasons and examples | 2 marks per point |\n| **Discuss** | Present different aspects with supporting detail | 2 marks per point |\n| **Distinguish** | Point out the differences between concepts | 2 marks per point |\n| **Evaluate** | Judge the value or effectiveness — give pros and cons | 2 marks per point |\n| **Recommend** | Suggest solutions with reasons | 2 marks per point |\n| **Analyse** | Break down into parts and examine in detail | 2 marks per point |\n\n**Tip:** If a question says "explain", you must give a reason or example — just naming or defining will not earn full marks.'),
  t(3, '### Essay Writing Tips\n\n**Structure of a Business Studies essay (40 marks):**\n\n**Introduction (max 2 marks):**\n- Define the topic or give context\n- Keep it short — 2-3 sentences\n\n**Body (max 32 marks):**\n- Use clear sub-headings\n- Use bullet points for clarity\n- Provide South African examples where possible\n- Cover ALL aspects the question asks about\n- Aim for 8-10 well-developed points\n\n**Conclusion (max 2 marks):**\n- Summarise the key points\n- Give a personal recommendation or opinion where appropriate\n\n**Layout/Insight marks (max 4 marks):**\n- Neat, logical presentation\n- Relevant sub-headings\n- Originality and depth of understanding\n- Real-world application\n\n**Common mistakes to avoid:**\n- Writing too much in the introduction (wasting time)\n- Not using sub-headings\n- Not answering what the question actually asks\n- Not providing examples\n- Repeating the same point in different words'),
  q(4, 'If a question asks you to "distinguish" between two concepts, what should you do?',
    ['Define both concepts', 'List examples of both', 'Point out the differences between them', 'Evaluate which one is better'], 2,
    'To distinguish means to point out the differences between two concepts. You should clearly explain how they differ from each other, ideally in a comparative format.'),
  t(5, '### Term 1 Revision: Business Environments\n\n**Micro environment (internal, can control):**\n- Vision, mission, goals, objectives\n- Organisational culture and structure\n- Resources (human, financial, physical, technological, information)\n- Eight business functions (general management, purchasing, production, marketing, financial, HR, admin, PR)\n- Quality concept (QC vs QA)\n\n**Market environment (external, limited influence):**\n- Consumers, suppliers, competitors, intermediaries, unions, regulators\n- The business can influence but not control these factors\n\n**Macro environment (external, no control):**\n- PESTLE: Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Legal, Ecological\n- SA-specific: load shedding, unemployment, crime, BBBEE, water scarcity\n- Business must ADAPT to these factors\n\n**Interrelationship:**\n- All three environments are interconnected\n- Changes in one affect the others\n- SWOT analysis connects internal (S, W) with external (O, T)'),
  t(6, '### Term 2 Revision: Sectors, Issues, Ownership\n\n**Business sectors:**\n- Primary (extraction), secondary (manufacturing), tertiary (services)\n- Formal vs informal sector; public vs private sector\n- Sectors are interdependent (value chain)\n- Tertiary sector is the largest in SA (over 65% of GDP)\n\n**Socio-economic issues:**\n- Poverty, inequality, unemployment, HIV/AIDS, crime, counterfeiting\n- Impact on businesses and strategies to address them\n- Inclusivity in the workplace\n\n**Social responsibility:**\n- CSR and CSI\n- BBBEE scorecard elements\n- Environmental responsibility\n- Responsibilities towards employees, customers, community, environment\n\n**Forms of ownership:**\n- Sole trader: unlimited liability, no legal personality, 1 owner\n- Partnership: 2-20 partners, unlimited liability, partnership agreement\n- CC: 1-10 members, limited liability, no new registrations since 2011\n- Private company (Pty) Ltd: limited liability, MOI, registered with CIPC\n- Public company (Ltd): shares on JSE, strict regulation\n- Co-operative: democratic, one member one vote, minimum 5 members\n- Companies Act 71 of 2008 governs companies'),
  t(7, '### Term 3 Revision: Ventures and Operations\n\n**Creative thinking and problem solving:**\n- Creative thinking vs problem solving\n- Problem-solving steps (7 steps)\n- Brainstorming rules and process\n- Force-field analysis (driving vs restraining forces)\n- Delphi technique (anonymous expert rounds)\n- SCAMPER technique\n\n**Business opportunities:**\n- Sources of opportunities (needs, trends, problems, technology)\n- Market research (primary vs secondary)\n- SWOT analysis for evaluating opportunities\n- Factors to consider (financial, market, personal, location, legal)\n- Sources of funding (personal, banks, government agencies, investors)\n- Steps to starting a business (opportunity → research → plan → register → launch)\n\n**Presentation skills:**\n- Verbal and non-verbal communication\n- Visual aids (types and rules for slides)\n- Presentation structure (introduction 10%, body 80%, conclusion 10%)\n- Formats: verbal, written, visual\n\n**Business plan:**\n- Components: executive summary, business description, market analysis, marketing plan, operations, management, financial plan\n- Executive summary written LAST\n- Break-even analysis: Fixed costs ÷ (Price − Variable cost)'),
  fb(8, 'The Grade 10 Business Studies exam has two papers: Paper 1 covers Business ___ and Paper 2 covers Business ___ and Roles.',
    ['Environments', 'Ventures'],
    'Paper 1 focuses on Business Environments (micro, market, macro, sectors, ownership, issues). Paper 2 covers Business Ventures and Roles (creative thinking, opportunities, presentations, business plan, teams).',
    ['Think about the two main themes of Grade 10 Business Studies.']),
  t(9, '### Term 4 Revision: Relationships and Teams\n\n**Relationships in the workplace:**\n- Types of business relationships (employer-employee, team, customer, supplier, community)\n- Factors influencing relationships (communication, respect, trust, fairness)\n- Prejudice, discrimination, and diversity\n- The role of values and beliefs\n- Constitutional rights in the workplace (Section 9 equality, Section 23 fair labour)\n\n**Team performance:**\n- Difference between a group and a team\n- Characteristics of effective teams\n- Conflict: causes, positive vs negative, resolution strategies\n- Five conflict resolution strategies (competing, accommodating, avoiding, compromising, collaborating)\n- Leadership styles (autocratic, democratic, laissez-faire)\n- Business objectives and alignment\n\n**Workplace legislation:**\n- LRA — employer-employee relations and unions\n- BCEA — minimum working conditions\n- EEA — equal opportunity and non-discrimination\n- OHS Act — safe working environment\n- Constitution — fundamental rights'),
  q(10, 'Which form of business ownership provides limited liability AND requires a Memorandum of Incorporation?',
    ['Sole proprietor', 'Partnership', 'Private company (Pty) Ltd', 'Co-operative'], 2,
    'A private company (Pty) Ltd provides limited liability to its shareholders and must file a Memorandum of Incorporation (MOI) with CIPC upon registration.'),
];

